2026-09-28      v1.1.0

                Adds support for PostgreSQL 19, which pgc now dumps,
                compares and migrates alongside 14 through 18, and fixes
                a long list of migration bugs that the work on it
                uncovered, most of them present in every earlier
                release. On PostgreSQL 19 the previous release could not
                dump a database at all once any database in the cluster
                held a subscription that takes its connection from a
                foreign server. Beyond that crash, the new publication,
                subscription, foreign-data wrapper, aggregate,
                constraint, generated-column and statistics features are
                now read, compared and written, in place wherever the
                server allows it.

                The fixes cover routines with SQL-standard bodies
                (dumped empty until now), migrations that failed or
                needed a second run (domain CHECK changes, rule changes,
                aggregates, partition-key changes, grantor chains,
                replica identity, owned sequences, publications,
                statistics), migrations that silently lost objects a
                DROP ... CASCADE took along, and a security issue: an
                object or role name containing a line break or a comment
                delimiter could end the comment pgc wrote it into and
                run as SQL when the migration was applied.

                Each dump now records the version of the server it was
                taken from, a dump whose schema pattern matches no
                schema (the source of a first deployment into an empty
                database) included. The migration is applied to the FROM
                database, so DDL that only a newer server accepts is
                emitted only when FROM is new enough, and otherwise
                falls back to a form every supported version accepts; a
                dump written by an earlier pgc carries no version and
                gets the fallback. pgc 1.1 reads every earlier dump, but
                refuses to compare a FROM dump it wrote against a TO
                dump written by pgc 1.0.x, whose missing information
                would turn into destructive statements; re-dump both
                sides after upgrading. Generated SQL changes for
                PostgreSQL 14 to 18 only where it was wrong before; each
                such change is listed below.

                Features:
                    - PostgreSQL 19 publications. FOR ALL SEQUENCES and
                      FOR ALL TABLES EXCEPT (TABLE ...) are dumped,
                      compared and created, the excluded tables written as
                      TABLE ONLY per catalog row as pg_dump 19 does. When
                      the migrated database is PostgreSQL 19 a
                      publication moves between FOR ALL TABLES, FOR ALL
                      SEQUENCES, both, a different EXCEPT list, and an
                      empty or listed publication in place with ALTER
                      PUBLICATION ... SET ALL ..., emptying a listed one
                      first; only leaving the all-objects kind for a list
                      or for nothing still needs a drop and recreate.
                      Older servers and dumps without a recorded server
                      version keep the drop and recreate.
                    - PostgreSQL 19 subscriptions that take their
                      connection from a foreign server: the server is
                      dumped, CREATE SUBSCRIPTION ... SERVER is emitted,
                      and a change of source becomes ALTER SUBSCRIPTION
                      ... SERVER or ... CONNECTION, never a drop and
                      recreate. When the owner changes as well, OWNER TO
                      runs before the SERVER switch, because PostgreSQL
                      checks the owner's user mapping, USAGE and
                      password when the source changes.
                    - The PostgreSQL 19 subscription options
                      retain_dead_tuples, max_retention_duration and
                      wal_receiver_timeout are dumped, created when not
                      at their default, and changed with one ALTER
                      SUBSCRIPTION ... SET. Turning retain_dead_tuples
                      off runs before a connection change and turning
                      it on runs after it, because PostgreSQL contacts
                      the publisher for both. PostgreSQL accepts that
                      change only once every worker of the subscription
                      has exited, so an enabled subscription is disabled
                      before anything else, the migration waits (at
                      most 60 seconds) until its workers are gone, and
                      every other change is made while it is disabled:
                      SET PUBLICATION, a new connection or server and an
                      owner change each restart a running worker, which
                      made the change fail in almost every run. The new
                      publication list is refreshed with REFRESH
                      PUBLICATION once the subscription is enabled
                      again. A subscription that ends disabled is enabled
                      for that refresh and disabled again. With
                      --use-single-transaction pgc warns that such a
                      change cannot succeed while the apply worker runs,
                      because the worker only sees the DISABLE once it
                      is committed.
                    - The PostgreSQL 19 foreign data wrapper CONNECTION
                      function is dumped and emitted as CONNECTION f
                      on CREATE, and as CONNECTION f or NO CONNECTION
                      on ALTER.
                    - All of the new values are hashed only when set,
                      so subscriptions and wrappers without them keep
                      their 1.0.x hashes, and older dumps load
                      unchanged.
                    - Added aggregate SUPPORT functions (PostgreSQL 19).
                      CREATE AGGREGATE ... SUPPORT = was read and
                      compared but never written, so the migration never
                      converged; it is now emitted, and adding or
                      removing one updates the aggregate in place.
                      Setting it needs superuser, as it does for
                      pg_dump; older servers ignore the option with a
                      warning.
                    - Added SET <list parameter> TO NULL (PostgreSQL
                      19), the empty list, which is stored as a bare
                      search_path=. It is emitted when the migrated
                      database is PostgreSQL 19 or later; for an older
                      server, or a FROM dump that does not record the
                      version, the portable SET search_path = '' is kept
                      as before.
                    - PostgreSQL 19: a CHECK constraint whose only
                      change is its enforceability is altered in place
                      with ALTER CONSTRAINT ... [NOT] ENFORCED instead
                      of being dropped and re-added. The statement
                      recurses to inheritance children and partitions,
                      and a parent's is emitted before its children's.
                      A table that drops and re-adds a column in the
                      same migration, or whose child keeps an enforced
                      copy of a constraint going NOT ENFORCED, keeps
                      the drop and re-add. PostgreSQL 18 rejects the
                      statement for CHECK constraints, so older servers
                      keep the drop and re-add too.
                    - PostgreSQL 19: a VIRTUAL generated column's
                      expression is changed with ALTER COLUMN ... SET
                      EXPRESSION, which keeps the column's position,
                      grants, dependent views, indexes and statistics.
                      On PostgreSQL 18 this is done only when no table
                      of the inheritance or partition family has a
                      CHECK constraint, counting tables the migration
                      creates. It is never done for a table that is an
                      explicit member of a publication, which
                      PostgreSQL rejects. SET EXPRESSION applies to the
                      table ONLY unless it is partitioned, so an
                      inheritance child keeps an expression of its own.
                      These choices follow the server version recorded
                      in the FROM dump. A dump that does not record one
                      gets the form every supported server accepts.
                    - Added support for PostgreSQL 19 extended
                      statistics on virtual generated columns. PostgreSQL
                      19 stores such a column as an expression, so every
                      statistics object that named one came out of pgc as
                      a CREATE STATISTICS the server rejects. They are now
                      created the way PostgreSQL deparses them, for
                      example ON a, (v), and restored when pgc drops and
                      re-adds the column they name.
                    - Added round-trip fixtures for PostgreSQL 19
                      syntax pgc already handles: the oid8 type in
                      columns, arrays, defaults, CHECK constraints, hash
                      indexes, domains, ranges, functions and views;
                      window IGNORE NULLS and RESPECT NULLS; JSON_ARRAY
                      over a subquery; the new heap storage parameters
                      autovacuum_parallel_workers and
                      log_autoanalyze_min_duration; the Polish stemmer,
                      the Esperanto configuration and the
                      pg_stash_advice extension. The dump gains two
                      optional fields, available_extensions and
                      standard_conforming_strings_off, which older
                      dumps may omit.

                Bug fixes: - A publication that switched from FOR ALL
                TABLES to a table list got ALTER PUBLICATION ... SET
                TABLE, which PostgreSQL rejects on every version ("is
                defined as FOR ALL TABLES"), so the migration failed and
                every later compare emitted it again. No ALTER leaves
                FOR ALL TABLES, so the publication is now dropped and
                recreated (commented out without --use-drop), as it
                already was in the other direction. FOR ALL TABLES to an
                empty publication, and a table list to an empty one,
                emitted nothing at all: the first is now recreated too,
                and the second emits ALTER PUBLICATION ... DROP TABLE
                ONLY for the members that are still there. - A table
                that the migration drops and recreates (a partition key
                change, for example) silently lost its publication
                membership, and the next compare reported it. With
                --use-drop, publications that list such a table now have
                their membership re-emitted even when nothing else about
                them changed, and tables the migration already dropped
                are left out of DROP TABLE lists, which would fail on
                them. - A publication member that is an inheritance
                parent was written without ONLY, so SET TABLE added its
                children as members too and every later compare emitted
                the same SET TABLE again, and a publication created on a
                parent with a row filter could not be recreated
                ("conflicting or redundant WHERE clauses"). Such members
                are now written TABLE ONLY, one per catalog row; other
                tables are written as before. - Publication row filters
                and column lists, FOR TABLES IN SCHEMA members
                (PostgreSQL 15+), publish_via_partition_root and
                publish_generated_columns (PostgreSQL 18+) were not
                dumped, so changes to them went unnoticed and a
                recreated publication came back without them. They are
                now dumped, compared and emitted: SET TABLE with the
                column list and WHERE clause, SET / DROP TABLES IN
                SCHEMA, and one SET (...) for the options, placed before
                the member change when it turns
                publish_via_partition_root on and after it otherwise. -
                Resolved a dump crash on PostgreSQL 19: a subscription
                created with SERVER has a NULL subconninfo, which the
                dump decoded into a string and panicked on. Because the
                subscription query also lacked a database filter, one
                such subscription anywhere in the cluster crashed every
                dump. The connection is now read NULL-safe. - Resolved
                subscriptions leaking between databases. pg_subscription
                is a shared catalog and the dump read all of it, so
                every database's subscriptions appeared in every dump,
                and two databases with same-named subscriptions produced
                arbitrary ALTERs. The query is now restricted to the
                current database, as pg_dump restricts it. - A failing
                subscription query is no longer mistaken for missing
                privileges. Only insufficient_privilege (SQLSTATE 42501)
                still degrades to an empty list with a warning; any
                other error fails the dump instead of silently dropping
                every subscription in the next compare. - FROM-only
                subscriptions are now dropped before every other
                statement, and FROM-only foreign servers and then
                foreign data wrappers after the subscriptions have been
                dropped or moved. On PostgreSQL 19 a subscription blocks
                DROP SERVER and DROP FOREIGN DATA WRAPPER even with
                CASCADE. Both sections appear only when something is
                dropped, so other output is unchanged. - Resolved
                changed aggregates failing to migrate. Every aggregate
                was written as a plain CREATE AGGREGATE, so any change
                that kept its signature - an INITCOND, a state size, a
                combine function - failed with "function already exists
                with same argument types" and was re-emitted on every
                later compare. Aggregates are now written as CREATE OR
                REPLACE AGGREGATE (PostgreSQL 12+), which updates them
                in place and keeps every view that uses them. - Resolved
                aggregates with a sort operator never being created.
                SORTOP was written as the stored operator name, SORTOP =
                pg_catalog.>, which does not parse; it is now written
                SORTOP = OPERATOR(pg_catalog.>), as pg_dump writes it. -
                Resolved routine and aggregate kind changes failing to
                migrate. A function replaced by an aggregate or a
                procedure of the same signature, or an ordered-set
                aggregate replaced by a hypothetical-set one, was sent
                to CREATE OR REPLACE, which PostgreSQL refuses ("cannot
                change routine kind"). Those changes, and a changed
                number of direct arguments, now drop the old routine
                first - with DROP ROUTINE when the kind changes, so a
                replayed migration still runs - and recreate the
                dependents the CASCADE removed. - Resolved the
                privileges of a dropped and recreated routine being
                lost. When a return type or routine kind change drops a
                routine and creates it again, its grants were compared
                with the ones the drop had already removed: explicit
                grants came back only on the next run, and a REVOKE
                EXECUTE FROM PUBLIC was silently lost, leaving the
                routine executable by everyone. They are now compared
                with the new routine's default privileges, so every
                grant and revoke is applied in the same migration. -
                Resolved an aggregate's PARALLEL SAFE or RESTRICTED
                marking never being migrated. It was compared but not
                written, so the aggregate was created PARALLEL UNSAFE
                and re-emitted on every compare. - Resolved a negative
                SSPACE or MSSPACE being silently dropped. PostgreSQL 19
                reads a negative state size as a state that can grow
                without bound (its own array_agg and string_agg use -1),
                and every supported version stores one, but pgc wrote
                only positive values and left negative ones out of the
                aggregate's hash, so the value was lost on creation and
                a change to or from it went undetected. Only such
                aggregates hash differently from pgc 1.0.x dumps, and
                are rewritten once. - Resolved routines with SET
                search_path = '' failing to migrate. The value is stored
                as search_path="" and was pasted back as SET search_path
                = "", which every PostgreSQL version rejects as a
                zero-length delimited identifier, so the routine was
                never created or updated. A list parameter (search_path,
                temp_tablespaces, ...) with an empty element, or with a
                name longer than 63 bytes, which was silently truncated,
                is now written element by element as string literals,
                the way pg_get_functiondef prints it (SET search_path TO
                '', 'public'), with numbers kept as numbers. Every other
                list is written exactly as before. - Resolved
                output_plugin_libraries lists being collapsed into one
                element. The parameter was not known to be a quoted
                list, so SET output_plugin_libraries = pgoutput,
                test_decoding was stored back as the single entry
                "pgoutput, test_decoding" and re-emitted on every
                compare. output_plugin_libraries and
                oauth_validator_libraries are now handled like
                search_path. - A change to a STORED generated column's
                expression produced a migration PostgreSQL could not
                run. pgc emitted DROP EXPRESSION followed by ALTER
                COLUMN ... ADD GENERATED ALWAYS AS (...) STORED, a
                statement no PostgreSQL version accepts, so the column
                was silently left as a plain column. The change is now
                ALTER COLUMN ... SET EXPRESSION when the migrated server
                is PostgreSQL 17 or newer, and a DROP COLUMN plus ADD
                COLUMN otherwise, with the column's indexes, constraints
                and policies restored afterwards. - A generated column
                that had to be dropped and re-added could not be while a
                view read it: PostgreSQL refused the DROP COLUMN and the
                migration never converged. Such views are now dropped
                first and recreated with the others. - A generated
                column that is dropped and re-added no longer starts
                with ALTER COLUMN statements the re-added column makes
                redundant. A type change among them failed outright,
                because PostgreSQL casts the old generation expression
                to the new type. A type change made together with an
                expression change is now always a drop and re-add. - A
                generated column whose new expression reads a column
                added in the same migration was changed before that
                column existed. Generated-column changes now come after
                the table's ADD COLUMNs and before its DROP COLUMNs. -
                An expression PostgreSQL deparses as ((a * 2) + (b * 3))
                lost both pairs of parentheses and came out as a syntax
                error, in CREATE TABLE and ADD COLUMN alike. Only a pair
                enclosing the whole expression is removed now. - A
                generated column changed across a classical inheritance
                family was dropped and re-added by every member, which
                PostgreSQL refused for the children ("cannot drop
                inherited column") and which left merged child copies
                with the old expression. The parent now drops and
                re-adds it once, removing the copies of the children
                that follow it in between. On a partition or such a
                child, a constraint on the column that the parent's drop
                already removed is dropped with IF EXISTS. - Classical
                inheritance was not part of the table order. A new child
                that sorted before its parent was created before the
                parent existed, and a removed parent that sorted before
                its child was dropped while the child still depended on
                it. Parents now come before their children on create and
                after them on drop. Tables already in a valid order keep
                their position. - A generated column whose expression
                differed from the other side only in how PostgreSQL
                deparsed an IN list was treated as changed whenever
                anything else about the column changed, and dragged a
                spurious drop and re-add along. - Removed the ALTER
                CONSTRAINT ... [NO] INHERIT that the CHECK constraint
                alter path could emit. PostgreSQL accepts it only for
                NOT NULL constraints, so a CHECK NO INHERIT change is
                always a drop and re-add. - Resolved CREATE STATISTICS
                failing for statistics on expressions (PostgreSQL 14 and
                later). The kind list always named "expressions", which
                is not a kind CREATE STATISTICS accepts, and univariate
                statistics - a single expression - were given a kind
                list at all, which PostgreSQL rejects. The kind list now
                leaves "expressions" out and is omitted for univariate
                statistics. - Resolved the ON list of extended
                statistics being rebuilt by splitting the definition on
                commas. A comma inside a literal changed the literal,
                and the FROM of EXTRACT(year FROM d) cut the list short,
                leaving a parenthesis open that made psql swallow every
                later statement of the migration into one syntax error.
                The ON list now comes from
                pg_get_statisticsobjdef_columns, and for dumps written
                by earlier versions it is cut out of the stored
                definition with a scan that respects quotes and
                parentheses. A change inside such an expression, which
                the split saw as no change at all, now drops and
                recreates the statistics. - Resolved extended statistics
                lost when PostgreSQL dropped them together with
                something pgc changed: a column pgc rewrites as DROP
                COLUMN + ADD COLUMN (a plain column becoming generated,
                a generated column whose change SET EXPRESSION cannot
                make), a generated column removed by DROP FUNCTION ...
                CASCADE - including statistics on the partitions of its
                table and statistics that are new in the target - a
                table dropped and recreated for a partition-key change,
                or a materialized view that is dropped and rebuilt. The
                dump now records which statistics depend on which
                columns, and the migration recreates them afterwards. -
                Resolved CREATE STATISTICS on a new materialized view
                running before the view was created. - Resolved
                statistics targets lost when a column the statistics
                read changes type or, on PostgreSQL 17 and later, its
                generation expression in place (ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE,
                SET EXPRESSION). PostgreSQL rebuilds the statistics
                object from its definition and resets its target; the
                migration now sets the target again. - Resolved explicit
                statistics targets (ALTER STATISTICS ... SET STATISTICS)
                being ignored on PostgreSQL 17 and later, where
                stxstattarget became a nullable smallint that pgc failed
                to read. - Resolved the drops of statistics that exist
                only in the source database coming out in a different
                order on every run. - Resolved a text search dictionary
                whose options changed being dropped with CASCADE and
                recreated. The CASCADE silently dropped every text
                search configuration that mapped the dictionary and,
                through them, every stored generated tsvector column and
                expression index built on them, and the next compare
                could not even put them back, because configurations are
                compared after tables. A change to options only is now
                one ALTER TEXT SEARCH DICTIONARY. It removes the options
                the target no longer has and lists every target option
                in target order, which leaves the stored options exactly
                equal to the target's. It runs before the tables, so a
                generated column or index added by the same migration is
                computed with the new options. The ALTER does not
                re-process values already stored, which the migration
                says in a comment and pgc repeats on stderr. A template
                change is still a drop and recreate, because PostgreSQL
                cannot alter it. PostgreSQL 19 made the options-only
                change common: it rewrote the Dutch stemmer, so keeping
                the old stems means moving a dictionary from language
                'dutch' to 'dutch_porter'. - Resolved a server, database
                or role set to standard_conforming_strings = off
                (possible up to PostgreSQL 18) being dumped with every
                backslash in defaults, CHECK constraints, index
                predicates and view bodies doubled. The doubled form
                produced spurious differences against every other dump,
                and stored an extra backslash when applied to a server
                with the setting on, which PostgreSQL 19 always is.
                Every pgc connection now sets it on, as pg_dump does.
                Each dump also records whether its server's clients
                default to off, and a migration for such a server starts
                with set standard_conforming_strings = on;, again as a
                pg_dump script does, so psql no longer reads 'C:\temp'
                as an escape string and stores a tab. Servers at the
                default see no change. - Resolved CREATE EXTENSION ...
                VERSION and ALTER EXTENSION ... UPDATE TO naming a
                version the migrated server cannot install. PostgreSQL
                19 ships btree_gist 1.9 as its only installable version,
                so pinning 1.8 from an older server failed, and so did
                every GiST index and exclusion constraint that needs it.
                Each dump now records the versions its server can
                install. When the FROM server cannot install the target
                version, a new extension is created at the server
                default and an impossible update is left out, each with
                a comment saying why. Dumps without the list, and every
                installable version, give the same SQL as before. -
                Resolved routines with a SQL-standard body (BEGIN ATOMIC
                ... END, or RETURN expr; PostgreSQL 14 and later) being
                dumped, compared and migrated with an empty body.
                PostgreSQL parses such a body when the routine is
                created and keeps it as a query tree in
                pg_proc.prosqlbody, leaving prosrc empty, and prosrc was
                all pgc read. Every one of them was replayed as AS $$$$:
                a function failed to create ("return type mismatch"), a
                procedure became an empty no-op without any error, and a
                change to the body was never detected, because the empty
                text hashed the same on both sides. The body is now read
                with pg_get_function_sqlbody under an empty search_path,
                as pg_dump reads it, so every name in it outside
                pg_catalog stays schema-qualified, public ones included,
                whatever search_path the dump session or the database
                has. It is emitted verbatim after every option with no
                AS. A changed body is replaced in place with CREATE OR
                REPLACE, which also converts between a quoted body and a
                SQL-standard one. A carriage return inside such a body
                belongs to a literal and is kept as it is. The dump
                marks these routines with a new optional is_sql_body
                field, which older dumps may omit. The hash and the
                emitted SQL of every other routine are unchanged; a
                SQL-standard routine compared from a pgc 1.0.x dump is
                re-created once with its real body. This also makes a
                routine body using PostgreSQL 19's INSERT ... ON
                CONFLICT DO SELECT migratable. - Resolved migrations
                failing, needing a second run or never converging around
                a routine with a SQL-standard body. Unlike a quoted
                body, such a body is a hard dependent of every relation,
                column, routine and operator it uses, and PostgreSQL
                resolves it when the routine is created, even with
                --guard-sql-routine-bodies. As a result a table,
                sequence or view it read could not be dropped or
                rebuilt, and a column it read could not be dropped or
                retyped. Dropping and re-creating a routine or operator
                it called silently took it along, and it could be
                created before a public routine or view it named, or
                before an operator or cast it applied. pgc now records
                these dependencies (a new optional sql_body_dependencies
                dump field) and drops such a routine, with the views and
                aggregates built on it, before any of those changes. It
                re-creates them afterwards with their grants and with
                the constraints, indexes, defaults and policies the drop
                took along. It orders them after the routines and views
                they use, and creates the ones that need a new operator
                or cast after it. - Resolved the clear script failing on
                routines with a SQL-standard body. One that reads a
                relation is now dropped together with the views, before
                that relation. A routine is dropped before the routines
                it calls, and an aggregate before its SQL-standard
                support function. - Resolved domain CHECK constraints
                and enum labels that changed on their own never reaching
                the migration. A type was hashed as soon as its pg_type
                row was read, before its domain constraints and enum
                labels were loaded, so the hash covered neither, and the
                comparer only looks at a type whose hash differs. A
                domain whose only change was a CHECK body, an added or
                dropped CHECK, or NOT VALID against validated - and an
                enum whose only change was a new label - produced no SQL
                at all, and the round-trip check could not notice,
                because the change was lost on both sides. Types are now
                hashed once both are loaded, and reading a dump file
                rehashes every type, so dumps written by pgc 1.0.x
                compare correctly with each other and with new ones; an
                old dump compared with a new one no longer reports
                constrained domains as "requires no supported changes".
                Such migrations now carry ALTER DOMAIN ... DROP/ADD
                CONSTRAINT and ALTER TYPE ... ADD VALUE statements that
                1.0.x silently left out. - Resolved domain CHECK
                constraints being added or validated too early. ADD
                CONSTRAINT and VALIDATE CONSTRAINT on a domain check
                every stored value of it, and they ran with the types,
                before the tables, so a table or column the same
                migration drops - still holding values only the old
                CHECK allowed - failed the migration, and a CHECK that
                calls a routine, operator or cast the migration creates
                failed because it did not exist yet. They now run in
                their own section after the tables, routines, operators
                and casts; dropping the old CHECK still happens first,
                with the types. - Resolved --use-single-transaction
                migrations that add an enum value and use it. PostgreSQL
                refuses a value added by ALTER TYPE ... ADD VALUE inside
                an open transaction ("unsafe use of new value"), so a
                column default or CHECK naming the new label failed the
                whole migration. The ADD VALUE statements, which carry
                IF NOT EXISTS and replay safely, now run before BEGIN; a
                label added there stays if the rest of the migration
                rolls back. Without --use-single-transaction nothing
                moves. - A domain CHECK that is NOT VALID in the
                migrated database and validated in the target is now
                validated in place with ALTER DOMAIN ... VALIDATE
                CONSTRAINT instead of being dropped and re-added, so it
                is no longer commented out under --use-drop false. With
                --use-drop false a commented-out domain statement is
                commented out on every line, so a CHECK whose body holds
                a line break no longer leaves the rest of the statement
                live. With --output-for-production, ALTER DOMAIN ...
                DROP CONSTRAINT is guarded with IF EXISTS, as ALTER
                TABLE already was, so a migration that re-creates a
                domain CHECK can be replayed after a partial run. - An
                enum whose shared labels are in a different order is now
                reported as such - PostgreSQL cannot reorder enum values
                - instead of as "requires no changes". A domain's
                constraints are hashed in name order, as the set they
                are, so the same constraints stored in a different order
                no longer read as a change; enum label order stays part
                of the hash. No hash in an existing dump changes because
                of this: every dump stores them in name order. -
                Resolved full grants mode never converging on privileges
                granted by a role other than the object's owner.
                PostgreSQL records who granted each privilege, and a
                REVOKE removes only the entries recorded under the role
                it runs as - the owner, when the owner or a superuser
                runs it. An entry a delegate granted (a role holding
                WITH GRANT OPTION; since PostgreSQL 19 also the role
                named by GRANT ... GRANTED BY) therefore survived the
                migration's plain REVOKE without an error, and the next
                compare emitted the same REVOKE again. Revoking a grant
                option such entries were granted under failed outright
                with "dependent privileges exist". pgc now revokes these
                entries in the grantor's name: REVOKE ... GRANTED BY on
                PostgreSQL 19 and later, and SET ROLE grantor / REVOKE /
                RESET ROLE on older servers or dumps that do not record
                a version. Entries granted under a grant option the
                migration takes away are revoked first, deepest in the
                chain first, and granted back by the owner when the
                target still wants them. These statements run at the top
                of the grants section, while each grantor still holds
                its grant option. The migrating role must be a
                superuser, or able to act as the grantor, and for SET
                ROLE the grantor needs USAGE on the object's schema.
                With --output-for-production each of them is wrapped in
                a DO block that runs it only while the entry is still
                recorded: once the grantor holds no privilege on the
                object any more, repeating the statement fails with
                "permission denied", which would have broken the promise
                that a production migration can be replayed. pgc reports
                on stderr, instead of emitting a statement bound to
                fail, any entry whose grantor no longer holds the grant
                option (PostgreSQL keeps such orphans and no REVOKE can
                reach them). On PostgreSQL 14 to 18 an entry whose
                grantor has since become a superuser still cannot be
                removed: a superuser's REVOKE acts as the object's
                owner, so the SET ROLE form removes nothing and the next
                compare emits it again. The GRANT side still ignores
                grantors, and migrations whose grants were all made by
                owners are unchanged byte for byte. - Resolved column
                privileges lost, or a table REVOKE rejected, in full
                grants mode when a table-level privilege is revoked.
                PostgreSQL applies a table-level REVOKE to every column
                as well, removing the column entries the same grantor
                gave the same grantee, but pgc compared the columns as
                if they were untouched. A column grant the target keeps
                - a reader keeping SELECT (note) while losing SELECT on
                the table - was gone after the migration and came back
                only on the next compare. Taking a table-level grant
                option away failed with "dependent privileges exist"
                when the column-level option it also removes had been
                used to grant the column to someone else. The columns
                are now planned against what the table statements leave:
                the column grants the target keeps are granted back in
                the same run, and the dependent column entries are
                revoked, in their grantor's name, before the table
                statement runs. - Resolved REPLICA IDENTITY USING INDEX
                not being supported at all. pgc dumped only
                relreplident, so it never knew which index a table used.
                A switch to USING INDEX emitted replica identity
                default, a no-op that came back on every run; a switch
                between two indexes was not detected; a table created by
                the migration, or an index rebuilt under the same name,
                silently lost it; and moving off it ran after a DROP NOT
                NULL on the key column, which PostgreSQL refuses (column
                is in index used as replica identity). The dump now
                records the index from pg_index.indisreplident, and its
                columns, in optional replica_identity_index and
                replica_identity_columns fields. Only the index name is
                hashed, and only when present, so every other table
                keeps its hash and older dumps still load. A table
                leaves USING INDEX first in its ALTER only when the
                migration drops or rebuilds that index, or drops,
                re-creates or clears the NOT NULL of one of its columns
                (and only with --use-drop). It returns last, once the
                index exists and those NOT NULLs are added and
                validated, which PostgreSQL 19 and 18.7 now require.
                Under --output-for-production the statement follows a
                CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY after commit. A partition on
                an index PostgreSQL creates from its parent is set after
                every table statement, in a block that warns instead of
                failing when no index of that name exists. A table left
                on USING INDEX after its index was dropped, which the
                server treats as NOTHING, is dumped as NOTHING. -
                Resolved rule changes that could not be applied. A
                changed rule was emitted exactly as pg_get_ruledef
                prints it, CREATE RULE ...;, with a second semicolon
                appended, and PostgreSQL rejected it with rule ...
                already exists. The change was never applied and every
                later compare emitted it again. A changed rule is now
                emitted as CREATE OR REPLACE RULE, which keeps the rule
                and its comment and can change the event, the condition,
                INSTEAD or ALSO and every action, including PostgreSQL
                19's ON CONFLICT DO SELECT. Every rule statement now
                ends in a single semicolon. - Resolved rules that were
                created too early or dropped too late. Rules were
                compared right after the tables, so a new rule on a new
                view, or one calling a new function, failed because
                neither existed yet. A removed rule was dropped after
                the tables, so a rule writing to a removed table blocked
                that table's DROP TABLE. Rules are now created after
                routines, views, operators and casts. Removed rules are
                dropped, in dump order, before any view or table DDL. -
                Resolved rules that were silently lost, or that stood in
                the way of other changes. A rule was never put back when
                its table was dropped or recreated (a partition key
                change, or a table turned into a view of the same name),
                when its view was rebuilt, or when a routine's DROP
                FUNCTION ... CASCADE took the rule itself, its view, or
                a view it reads. A rule reading a column or
                foreign-table column being retyped made ALTER COLUMN ...
                TYPE fail. A rule reading a table, foreign table or
                sequence being dropped blocked the drop. Dumps now
                record which relations, columns and routines each rule
                depends on. Such a rule is dropped before the change and
                recreated with CREATE OR REPLACE RULE after it, and a
                comment the target no longer has is cleared. A rule
                whose own table or view goes away is put back even when
                the dump was written by an older pgc. - Resolved DROP
                RULE and COMMENT ON RULE for names that need quoting.
                The dump stores these names already quoted, and pgc
                quoted them again, so "My Rule" became """My Rule""".
                That is another name: the drop did nothing and was
                emitted again on every run, and the comment failed. - In
                production mode new rules are emitted as CREATE OR
                REPLACE RULE, so the migration can be run again. Each
                rule in the dump gains an optional depends_on field,
                which older dumps may omit. Rule hashes are unchanged. -
                Resolved serial detection turning any column with an
                owned sequence and a nextval default into a serial
                column. The type fell back to serial for every column
                that was not an integer, so a numeric, domain or
                PostgreSQL 19 oid8 column was created as integer with an
                integer sequence, and every later compare failed on
                MAXVALUE out of range. The same shortcut was taken for a
                sequence of another type than its column, a custom or
                renamed sequence name, a nullable column, changed
                sequence options, a sequence comment or a changed
                sequence persistence. None of these converged or kept
                the second compare empty. A column of a table that
                already existed, a partition or a typed table also took
                it: the sequence was skipped as created by the column,
                but ADD COLUMN, SET DEFAULT, PARTITION OF and OF type
                never create it, so it was missing or the migration
                failed. A serial whose sequence another new table used
                in its default failed when that table was created first.
                The serial pseudo-types are now used only when they
                recreate the sequence exactly in a new plain table, and
                genuine serial, bigserial and smallserial columns are
                emitted as before. Every other owned sequence is created
                with its own options before the tables and linked to its
                column with ALTER SEQUENCE ... OWNED BY after them. -
                Resolved a new sequence owned by a column losing its
                ownership. When it was created on its own, no OWNED BY
                was ever emitted, and the second compare could not
                notice because the sequence hash does not include
                ownership. - Resolved a sequence owned by an identity
                column that is not its identity sequence being treated
                as the identity sequence. A new one was skipped as
                created by the column and never created, and one removed
                from the target was never dropped. - Resolved an SQL
                injection through the names pgc writes into the comments
                of a migration. Object and role names went verbatim into
                the /* Table: ... */ headers, into the -- use_drop=false
                notes and into the statements commented out for review,
                and PostgreSQL allows any character in a name: a line
                feed or a bare carriage return ends a -- comment, */
                ends a block comment and /* opens a nested one. A view
                named v<newline>drop table t; -- whose columns changed
                made the applied migration drop t. A table named x*/
                create table y(); /* created y. An extension owned by a
                role with such a name ran its payload even with
                --use-comments false. A name containing /* swallowed the
                rest of the script. Names written into a -- comment now
                have their line breaks replaced by spaces, as pg_dump
                does. Names written into a block comment have */ and /*
                split apart. A commented-out statement carries -- in
                front of every physical line, the ones after a bare
                carriage return included. The comment stripper behind
                --use-comments false and the production rewriter now end
                a -- comment at a bare carriage return, as PostgreSQL's
                lexer does. Dump hashes are unchanged, and the output
                for ordinary names is byte-for-byte the same. - Resolved
                materialized views being named without quote_ident. pgc
                read their schema, name and owner raw from pg_matviews,
                so every statement it wrote for one (CREATE, ALTER ...
                OWNER TO, DROP, the clear script) used the bare catalog
                name. A mixed-case view "MyMv" was created as mymv and
                never converged, its column comments were lost from the
                dump, and a name holding SQL ran it. They are now read
                through quote_ident like every other object. A dump
                written by pgc 1.0.x has its materialized-view names
                quoted on load, so it still matches a current dump of
                the same view; names that need no quotes keep their
                hashes. - Resolved the guarded REPLICA IDENTITY block
                for a partition always being dollar-quoted as $pgc$. A
                partition or index name containing $pgc$ ended the quote
                and ran the rest of itself as SQL. The block now uses a
                tag its body does not contain ($pgc$, else $pgc1$, ...).
                - Resolved foreign-table option changes never applying.
                pgc sent the whole new option list as OPTIONS (k 'v',
                ...), which means ADD, so changing an option's value or
                adding one next to an existing option failed with option
                "k" provided more than once. Options are now changed key
                by key: SET for a changed value, DROP for a removed key
                and ADD for a new one. A change of order alone emits
                nothing. - Resolved the silent loss of objects a
                routine's DROP ... CASCADE takes along. pgc drops a
                routine with CASCADE ahead of the table changes when its
                SQL-standard body reads a column being retyped, and in
                the routine phase when its return type, arguments or
                kind change. PostgreSQL then removes everything that
                depends on it, and pgc used to put back only table
                constraints, indexes, column defaults, generated
                columns, policies, views and rules. The migration
                applied cleanly and lost the domain CHECKs, the triggers
                whose WHEN clause calls the routine (and the enabled
                state and comment of their partition clones), the
                extended statistics and the indexes on materialized
                views calling it, the defaults of foreign-table columns
                calling it, and the routines whose argument defaults or
                SQL-standard bodies call it. Worse, a domain whose
                DEFAULT calls the routine was dropped whole, together
                with every table column of that type and the data in it.
                All of these are now put back after the routines, from
                the TO dump, and a domain default is detached right
                before the drop and set again afterwards. - Resolved
                migrations that failed because the table changes created
                objects calling a routine pgc had dropped ahead of them.
                Constraints, indexes, policies and column defaults
                calling such a routine are now left out of the table
                changes and created once the routine is back, and a
                routine the routine phase's CASCADE takes along through
                an argument default is re-created there instead of being
                dropped before the tables. - Added safeguards for a DROP
                ... CASCADE that pgc could not undo. A routine whose
                CASCADE would take a partitioned table through its
                partition key, a range or base type (and every column of
                it), a foreign-data wrapper, or an operator or cast is
                no longer dropped ahead of the change it is in the way
                of. The routine phase drops such a routine without
                CASCADE. Neither is a routine that a new column's
                default, a new column's domain default or a generated
                column needs while the tables change dropped ahead of
                them. The migration says so in a comment and on stderr,
                and fails on PostgreSQL's dependency check, as it did in
                1.0.27, instead of silently losing the table or the
                data. - Resolved a CHECK constraint that the migration
                relaxes or drops still being checked by the rewrite of
                the column it reads, which failed with "check constraint
                ... is violated by some row" - usually the very reason
                the CHECK was changing. A partition's or an inheritance
                child's own CHECK was relaxed or dropped in that table's
                alter, after the parent's recursing ALTER COLUMN ...
                TYPE (or a partitioned parent's SET EXPRESSION) had
                already rewritten its rows; such relaxes and drops now
                run ahead of every table alter (on PostgreSQL 19 an
                in-place ALTER CONSTRAINT ... NOT ENFORCED as well), and
                the re-added or tightened CHECK still follows the
                rewrite. - Resolved grants mode revoking column
                privileges that the migration's own table DDL had
                already removed, and losing the ones the target still
                wanted. A generated column that is dropped and re-added,
                and every column of a table that is dropped and
                recreated, start with no privileges, but they were
                diffed against the source column's ACL: the target's
                column grants were not issued again until the next run,
                and an entry a delegate had granted was revoked in the
                delegate's name, which failed with "permission denied
                for column" (and, with --use-single-transaction, rolled
                back the whole migration on every run). These columns
                are now planned from an empty ACL, including a
                partition's or an inheritance child's copy that the
                parent's drop and add re-creates. In addonly mode a
                dropped and recreated table's own grants are now issued
                again as well, where before they were taken for present
                and lost until the next run. A partition whose column
                type changes through its parent is not dropped, and
                neither is a table whose DROP TABLE --use-drop leaves
                commented out: their table privileges are diffed against
                their real ACL instead of the default privileges, and
                entries a delegate granted on such a partition are
                revoked in the delegate's name, where before a plain
                REVOKE left them in place. - Resolved a compare of a
                FROM dump written by this version with a TO dump written
                by pgc 1.0.x producing a destructive migration, even
                when both dumps were taken from the same, unchanged
                database. pgc 1.0.x leaves out, or holds wrongly, what
                it could not read, and the gaps were taken as the state
                to reach: tables whose names start with pg but not pg_
                (pgbench_accounts) were dropped, SQL-standard routines
                were replaced with an empty body, so a procedure
                silently became a no-op, publication row filters, column
                lists, TABLES IN SCHEMA members and options were
                stripped, which published columns the list had kept out,
                and the subscriptions of the cluster's other databases,
                which pgc 1.0.x wrote into every dump, were created,
                connecting to their publishers. No safe migration can be
                derived from such a pair, so compare now refuses it: pgc
                exits non-zero, writes no script and says to re-dump TO
                with this version. The --config chain dumps both sides
                itself and never mixes versions. - Resolved a FROM dump
                written by pgc 1.0.x of a PostgreSQL 14 to 18 database
                whose clients default to standard_conforming_strings =
                off silently corrupting backslash literals. Such a dump
                holds every backslash doubled and does not record the
                setting, so against a TO dump written by this version
                every default, CHECK constraint and view holding a
                backslash looked changed and was rewritten in standard
                form without set standard_conforming_strings = on;, and
                psql stored 'C:\temp' as C:, a tab and emp. Such a
                migration now starts with the SET whatever the FROM
                server's default; on a server at the default it changes
                nothing, and it is still left out of an otherwise empty
                migration. - Resolved a FROM dump written by pgc 1.0.x
                silently leaving a TO subscription out of the migration.
                pgc 1.0.x wrote the subscriptions of every database in
                the cluster into each dump, so a subscription of the
                same name in another database counted as already present
                and was never created, and one that differed was altered
                from the other database's definition. Against a TO dump
                written by this version every TO subscription is now
                created, which fails with subscription already exists,
                before the publisher is contacted, where FROM has it;
                subscriptions only FROM lists are dropped once each with
                DROP SUBSCRIPTION IF EXISTS. - A FROM dump written by
                pgc 1.0.x compared with a TO dump written by this
                version is compared as before, with a warning on stderr
                and a comment at the top of the script: the migration
                re-applies what pgc 1.0.x could not read, re-creating
                SQL-standard routines and setting publications and
                replica identities again, and it creates the tables pgc
                1.0.x could not see and every subscription, which stderr
                lists and which fail with already exists where FROM has
                them. Production output skips such a table's CREATE
                TABLE, but the constraints added to it separately still
                fail and any other difference in that table is not
                applied. Re-dump FROM to avoid all of this. Two dumps
                written by pgc 1.0.x compare exactly as before. -
                Resolved the partitions of a recreated table going
                missing. A partition-key change, or a type change on a
                partition-key column, drops the partitioned table, and
                DROP TABLE takes every partition with it, but pgc only
                created again the partitions whose own definition had
                changed. One that kept its bound - the DEFAULT
                partition, or every partition when a HASH or RANGE key
                moves to another column - was gone until a second run,
                together with its indexes, grants, statistics, triggers,
                rules and publication membership, and until then every
                insert into its range failed. Every partition the drop
                takes, at any depth, is now created again in the same
                migration; a partition only 'to' has waits until its
                recreated parent is back, also when its name needs
                quoting; and the views, SQL-body routines and rules
                reading such a partition are dropped first and recreated
                after. The parent's row triggers, which PostgreSQL 15
                and later lists on every partition, are no longer
                created a second time on a recreated partition, where
                they failed with "already exists"; a partition's own
                trigger that only shares its name with one of the
                parent's is still created. - Resolved a table that 'to'
                attaches to, or moves under, a recreated partitioned
                table going with it. Taken in name order, a table that
                came before its new parent was attached to the old one,
                and the DROP TABLE that followed took it along, rows and
                all, without an error. It is now attached once its new
                parent is back, and so is a recreated table that moves
                under another one, after which its own partitions are
                created again. - Resolved a recreated table with a
                serial column not being created at all. DROP TABLE also
                dropped the sequence the column owns, the CREATE TABLE
                that followed named it in its nextval default and failed
                with "relation ... does not exist", and the migration
                left neither the table, its partitions nor the sequence
                behind. The sequence is now detached with ALTER SEQUENCE
                ... OWNED BY NONE right before the DROP TABLE and owned
                by its column again once the tables are back, which also
                keeps its current value, owner and privileges. Identity
                columns were not affected. - Resolved a recreated table
                that another table's foreign key references failing the
                whole migration. DROP TABLE refused to run ("other
                objects depend on it"), every CREATE after it failed
                with "already exists", the views and routines dropped
                ahead of it came back on the old table, and the
                migration never converged. Foreign keys referencing a
                recreated table or one of its partitions are now dropped
                before the table changes and added back after them, NOT
                VALID and then validated under --output-for-production;
                one the referencing table changes or drops in the same
                migration is dropped only once. This covers a key that
                names a public table without its schema, as PostgreSQL
                writes it when the search path reaches it, and a key
                referencing a partitioned table one of whose
                sub-partitions is recreated, which PostgreSQL copies
                onto that sub-partition. The recreated table's own
                foreign keys, which CREATE TABLE leaves out and which
                were silently lost until a second run, are added back
                too. Rows still pointing at the lost rows of the
                recreated table make adding a key back fail, as for any
                foreign key to an empty table. A foreign table that is a
                partition of a recreated table is still only created
                again by a second run, and then as a standalone foreign
                table: pgc does not know foreign tables as partitions. -
                Resolved ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT lowercasing
                quoted identifiers. A constraint whose definition names
                a mixed-case schema, table or column, such as a foreign
                key referencing "Sales"."Orders"("Id"), was emitted as
                "sales"."orders"("id") and failed with "does not exist".
                Quoted identifiers now keep their case; everything
                outside them and string literals is lowercased as
                before. - Resolved --output-for-production adding a
                foreign key to a partitioned table NOT VALID on a server
                older than PostgreSQL 18, which refuses it ("cannot add
                NOT VALID foreign key on partitioned table") and so
                aborted a single-transaction migration. Such a foreign
                key is now added in one statement there, and when the
                'from' dump does not record its server version. -
                Resolved the objects a routine's DROP ... CASCADE took
                through a view. When a routine is dropped, or its return
                type, arguments or kind change, the routine phase drops
                it with CASCADE. PostgreSQL then removes every view
                calling it and, with each view, everything reading that
                view: other views, materialized views with their data,
                indexes and statistics, routines with a SQL-standard
                body, and rules. pgc put back only the views calling the
                routine, so the rest was missing until the next compare,
                and a rule on a lost view failed to be re-created. A
                view calling such a routine that something reads is now
                dropped ahead of the table changes together with its
                readers. All of them are re-created after the routine in
                dependency order, with their owners, comments, indexes,
                statistics, rules and privileges. The dump now records,
                per view, the routines its definition depends on, and
                the call is matched on those: a view calling an
                unchanged overload, or a built-in of the same name, is
                left in place. A view that a plain DROP VIEW could not
                remove (a routine with its row type in its signature, a
                policy reading it, a column of its row type) is left to
                the CASCADE as before, and so is every view of a FROM
                dump written by pgc 1.0.x. - Resolved the privileges of
                a view re-created after a routine's DROP ... CASCADE
                took it. They were compared with the view's FROM
                privileges, which the CASCADE had removed. Its grants
                came back only on the next compare, and a role the
                schema's default privileges let in kept its access
                although the view revoked it. Such a view is now planned
                like a dropped one, from the default privileges. A view
                matched only by the routine's name, which the CASCADE
                does not take, keeps its privileges and its
                grantor-named revokes. - Resolved the privileges of a
                routine the migration creates - new, or dropped and
                re-created - ignoring the default privileges for
                functions (ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES ... ON FUNCTIONS).
                What a schema's defaults granted and the routine revokes
                stayed granted. A routine granting EXECUTE to PUBLIC
                under a database-wide REVOKE ... FROM PUBLIC default
                lost that grant. Each lasted until the next compare. The
                routine's privileges are now planned from every ACL the
                default privileges can give it, for each role that may
                create it. - Resolved a routine with no explicit
                privileges in TO losing PUBLIC's EXECUTE. Compared with
                an explicit FROM ACL, the NULL ACL was read as no
                privileges at all. The migration therefore revoked
                EXECUTE from PUBLIC whenever the FROM routine had other
                grants or had its default spelled out, and never granted
                it back where FROM had revoked it. The next compare saw
                nothing to do. The NULL ACL is now read as the default
                it stands for. - Resolved role names that need quotes
                being written bare in GRANT and REVOKE. aclitem text
                leaves a mixed-case or reserved-word role unquoted, so
                the statement named a different, lower-case role, or did
                not parse. Object, column and default-privilege grants
                now quote every such grantee, as the grantor-named
                revokes already did, and a mixed-case owner is
                recognised in its own ACL. - Resolved subscription
                publication names being written unquoted in CREATE
                SUBSCRIPTION and ALTER SUBSCRIPTION ... SET PUBLICATION.
                The dump keeps the names as the catalog holds them, so
                subscription hashes are unchanged. - Resolved REVOKE
                EXECUTE / USAGE ... FROM PUBLIC never being migrated for
                routines and types whose source ACL is NULL, the default
                privileges. A NULL ACL was compared as "no privileges"
                instead of as the PUBLIC EXECUTE or USAGE it grants, and
                the reverse change took PUBLIC's privilege away. An ACL
                with everything revoked is now told apart from NULL. -
                Resolved new and recreated routines and types being
                given the wrong privileges when the source database has
                default privileges (ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE
                ...). Only the creating role's defaults apply, and the
                role running the migration is not known, so the
                migration now revokes whatever the target lacks and
                grants whatever it holds. A range type the migration
                drops and recreates gets its privileges back the same
                way. A multirange type gets no GRANT or REVOKE, which
                PostgreSQL refuses. - Resolved a range type whose
                privileges alone changed being dropped with CASCADE and
                recreated, taking every column of that type with it. A
                type whose only difference is its ACL is now left to the
                GRANT and REVOKE statements. - Resolved ALTER
                SUBSCRIPTION ... SET PUBLICATION failing on a disabled
                subscription, or inside --use-single-transaction. The
                refresh is now skipped there and run with REFRESH
                PUBLICATION after the subscription is enabled, or after
                commit. - Resolved column drops, column type changes,
                SET UNLOGGED and, on PostgreSQL 19, ATTACH PARTITION
                failing on tables a publication row depends on through a
                column list, a row filter or an EXCEPT entry. Those rows
                are now taken out before the tables are changed and put
                back afterwards, including in a publication the
                migration keeps unchanged. A row that cannot come back,
                such as a table made UNLOGGED, gets a note and a
                warning. - Resolved a migration that failed on every run
                when a column read by a generated column changed type.
                PostgreSQL refuses ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE while a
                generated column (STORED or VIRTUAL) reads the column,
                on every version, even when the generated column itself
                does not change. The generated column is now dropped
                before the retype and re-added from its target
                definition after it, and what the drop takes along is
                restored: its indexes, CHECK constraints, extended
                statistics, the views reading it and its column
                privileges. A generated column that becomes a plain
                column drops its expression first and keeps its values;
                one the target no longer has is dropped first. When both
                a column and a generated column reading it are dropped,
                the generated column now goes first; the old order
                failed on PostgreSQL 15 and later, and on 14 the second
                drop found no column. - Resolved type changes in
                classical inheritance (INHERITS) families. A child
                re-emitted ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE for the column it
                inherits, which PostgreSQL refuses ("cannot alter
                inherited column"); the parent's statement already
                recurses to it. A generated column of the child reading
                that column made the parent's statement fail as well.
                The child now leaves the retype to its parent, and its
                generated columns are dropped ahead of every table alter
                and re-added in its own alter. - Resolved column drop +
                add migrations that PostgreSQL refused because other
                objects depend on the column: a policy using it, a
                trigger naming it in UPDATE OF or its WHEN clause, or
                another table's foreign key referencing a key on it.
                This covers a generated column re-created around a
                retype and one whose expression change needs a drop +
                add. These objects are now dropped ahead of the table
                changes and put back afterwards, triggers in their
                enabled state. On PostgreSQL 18 and later a named NOT
                NULL constraint on such a column is re-added under its
                name; the auto-named one the plain NOT NULL created made
                the restore fail. Constraint definitions also keep the
                case of quoted identifiers: a unique key or foreign key
                on a column such as "Code" was written as "code" and
                failed. - Resolved extended statistics that were dropped
                and created again on every compare. PostgreSQL prints
                the plain columns of a statistics object in physical
                column order, so the same object printed differently on
                two tables whose columns sat in another order - for
                instance after a column was dropped and re-added. Each
                re-create threw away the collected statistics until the
                next ANALYZE. The plain columns are now compared as a
                set and the expressions in order. Stored hashes are
                unchanged. - Resolved CHECK constraint changes in
                classical inheritance families that PostgreSQL refused.
                When a child's copy of a CHECK is both its own and
                inherited, and it has to be dropped and re-added (an
                enforceability change before PostgreSQL 19 or against a
                FROM dump without a version, or an expression change on
                any version) or becomes only inherited, the migration
                failed with "cannot drop inherited constraint", "already
                exists" or "conflicts with NOT ENFORCED constraint", and
                the tightening case could leave the parent without its
                CHECK. Every copy in the family is now dropped, parents
                first, before any is re-added. An ancestor whose copy
                does not change drops and re-adds it as it is. -
                Resolved partitioned tables with a row trigger whose
                migration failed. PostgreSQL clones such a trigger onto
                every partition and creates and drops the clones with
                it, but the clones were dumped as the partitions' own
                triggers: every new or re-created partitioned table got
                a CREATE TRIGGER per partition that failed with "already
                exists", and dropping the table or changing the trigger
                emitted a DROP TRIGGER on a clone that PostgreSQL
                refuses. A clone is now never created or dropped. Only
                an enabled state or comment of its own is kept, and set
                once the parent's trigger is in place. On PostgreSQL 14,
                where clones are internal, those states were lost and
                are now migrated. A partition whose parent lies outside
                the dumped schemas keeps every clone in the dump,
                compared with the other side's, and a new such partition
                gets its clones' states set. Dumps written by pgc 1.0.x
                are read the same way. - Resolved ATTACH PARTITION
                failing with "trigger already exists" when the table had
                a trigger of its own named like a row trigger of its new
                parent. That trigger is now dropped before the attach,
                which clones the parent's. - Resolved the clear command
                dropping objects in an order PostgreSQL refuses, so the
                round-trip fixture databases could not be cleared
                without --use-cascade. The script kept fixed sections -
                views, tables, routines, sequences, types, extensions,
                schemas - with only a few orderings of its own. An
                aggregate went before its state function only when that
                function had a SQL-standard body. A domain came after
                the function its CHECK calls, and domains, classic
                INHERITS children and tables whose rules read a view
                kept their dump order. Operators, casts, collations and
                text search objects were never dropped, which left the
                schema non-empty, and neither was anything that depended
                on a dropped extension, such as a foreign-data wrapper
                using the postgres_fdw handler. The clear command now
                reads the dependencies PostgreSQL records (pg_depend)
                once, after it has read the schema, and moves a drop
                only as far as one of them requires, so a schema without
                such dependencies gets the script it always had. A
                dependency cycle - a table whose CHECK, default,
                trigger, rule, policy or index calls a function whose
                SQL-standard body reads that same table - is broken by
                dropping that part of the table first; a stored
                generated column loses only its expression (DROP
                EXPRESSION), so a view reading the column does not block
                it. On PostgreSQL 14, where dropping the function drops
                the generated column with it, whatever reads the column
                goes first. Event triggers are dropped before anything
                else, so one that logs DDL into a table of the schema no
                longer fails every drop after that table is gone.
                Operators, casts, collations and text search
                configurations and dictionaries are dropped with their
                schema. A foreign-data wrapper, server, user mapping,
                subscription or event trigger, which belongs to no
                schema, is dropped only when it depends on something the
                script drops inside the selected schemas and nothing
                outside them depends on it: an extension in public that
                is read although public is not selected pulls in
                nothing, and a server that a foreign table in another
                schema uses is left in place, with a warning, together
                with its user mappings. A SQL-standard routine that
                reads only relations the script leaves alone is again
                dropped after the tables whose defaults call it, as in
                1.0.27. - Resolved an aggregate's SUPPORT clause being
                sent to a FROM server older than PostgreSQL 19, which
                accepts it with a WARNING and stores nothing, so every
                later compare re-created the aggregate. When the FROM
                dump records an older server, the clause is left out
                with a comment in the script and a warning on stderr,
                and an aggregate that differs only in its support
                function gets just the comment. A FROM dump without a
                version, one written by pgc 1.0.x, gets the clause as
                before. - Resolved a generated column whose type and
                expression both change emitting, with --use-drop off, a
                live ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE ahead of the commented-out
                drop and re-add. PostgreSQL computes that type change
                from the old expression, so it failed ("cannot be cast
                automatically") or did work the re-add redoes. It is now
                part of the commented-out drop and re-add. - Removed the
                probe of pg_constraint.connullsnotdistinct, a column no
                PostgreSQL version has (UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT is
                pg_index.indnullsnotdistinct, PostgreSQL 15+, which
                pg_get_constraintdef already renders), together with the
                dead select behind it. - Documented exactly what
                --use-drop off does and does not guarantee: no DROP
                statement and no ALTER ... DROP column, constraint,
                default, NOT NULL, identity or expression clause runs,
                but comment removals, REVOKE under --grants-mode full,
                taking tables out of a publication, DROP MAPPING,
                OPTIONS (DROP ...) and DISABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY still
                do, and the statements that depend on a suppressed drop
                still run and fail. - Resolved tables whose name starts
                with pg followed by any character other than an
                underscore (pgbench_accounts, pg1_orders) being left out
                of every dump. The tables query filtered tablename not
                like 'pg_%', and _ is a LIKE wildcard, so such a table
                was never compared, never created and never dropped,
                silently. The filter now matches a literal underscore. -
                Resolved a column whose quoted name contains a space
                breaking every migration that created its table. CREATE
                TABLE removed each column's name by cutting its
                definition at the first space, so "Col X" integer came
                out as "Col X" X" integer: an unterminated quoted
                identifier, which made psql read the rest of the
                migration into it and fail it as one syntax error. The
                name is now removed by its exact length.
                    - Resolved the objects a routine's DROP ... CASCADE
                      took along with a stored generated column calling
                      it, for instance when the routine's return type or
                      parameter names changed, or when it had a
                      SQL-standard body dropped ahead of the tables. The
                      generated column was re-added, but not the views
                      reading it (or the views over those), the
                      SQL-standard routines and rules reading it, its
                      column privileges, the triggers naming it in UPDATE
                      OF or reading it in WHEN, the publication rows whose
                      column list or row filter used it, the objects only
                      the target schema had on it, or a partition's own
                      objects on its copy. A table could silently drop out
                      of a publication. When the same migration also
                      dropped such a view for a type change, the view was
                      re-created before the column was back and the
                      migration failed with "column does not exist". All
                      of these are now dropped ahead where needed and put
                      back after the column is re-added. The table phase
                      also no longer runs ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE on such a
                      column first: that was redundant on every version,
                      and PostgreSQL 14 refused it ("found unexpected
                      dependency for column"), which aborted a
                      --use-single-transaction migration.
                    - Resolved column defaults, CHECK constraints and
                      policies calling a routine that reads such a
                      re-added generated column being put back before that
                      routine was created again: the migration failed with
                      "function does not exist", or the routine's own
                      DROP ... CASCADE silently took them again. They are
                      now put back after it.
                    - Resolved extended statistics on a materialized view
                      being created before the view when the view was
                      created together with routines held back until after
                      the operators and casts.
                    - Resolved a migration that failed on every run when
                      a publication's column list or row filter used a
                      generated column dropped and re-added because a
                      column it reads changed type. Those publication rows
                      are now released before the tables and restored
                      after them, on partitions too.
                    - Resolved a partition's own index, CHECK, UNIQUE key
                      (with the foreign keys referencing it), extended
                      statistics and RLS policy on a generated column
                      being lost when its partitioned parent dropped and
                      re-added the column around a type change. They are
                      now restored after the table changes.
                    - Resolved a type change of a stored generated column
                      that never applied on PostgreSQL 14 when its
                      expression used a user-defined function or operator:
                      PostgreSQL 14 records that use on the column itself
                      and refuses ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE. The dump of a
                      PostgreSQL 14 server now marks such columns, and
                      the migration drops and re-adds them, restoring
                      what depends on them. Other versions and pgc 1.0.x
                      dumps are unchanged.
                    - Resolved two PostgreSQL 14 failures when such a
                      generated column is made a plain column. With a type
                      change as well, the migration never applied. When
                      the migration also dropped the function the
                      expression called, the column and its data were
                      silently deleted, since PostgreSQL 14 keeps the
                      column depending on the function after DROP
                      EXPRESSION. Such a column is now rewritten with its
                      values kept, and what depends on it is restored.
                    - Resolved a type change of a column used by an RLS
                      policy, by a trigger's UPDATE OF list or by its
                      WHEN clause, which failed on every run ("cannot
                      alter type of a column used in a policy/trigger
                      definition"). This includes another table's policy
                      reading the column through a subquery. Such
                      policies and triggers are now dropped ahead of the
                      table changes, or created only after them.
                    - Resolved an SQL injection through column
                      collation names. pgc wrote a column's collation
                      as collate "<name>" from the raw catalog name, so
                      a " in the name closed the identifier and the
                      rest of the name ran as SQL when the migration
                      was applied, even inside a single transaction.
                      The collation was also written without its
                      schema: a collation outside search_path failed
                      with "does not exist", and a same-named one
                      earlier on the path was bound silently. A
                      collation outside pg_catalog is now qualified and
                      quoted as quote_ident does (collate app.ci);
                      pg_catalog collations keep collate "C".
                    - Resolved a change of a column's collation alone
                      never being migrated: the collation was not part
                      of the table hash. It is now, for columns that
                      have one, and the change is applied with ALTER
                      COLUMN ... TYPE ... COLLATE; on a partition-key
                      column, which PostgreSQL cannot retype, the table
                      is dropped and recreated as for a type change
                      there, and the script says it is the collation.
                      Tables without a collated column keep their
                      hashes, and a pgc 1.0.x dump has its collated
                      tables rehashed on load so it still matches a
                      current dump.
                    - Resolved collations the target adds being created
                      after the tables: a column moved to a new
                      collation, a new table, a new column or a domain
                      using one failed with "collation ... does not
                      exist", and a single-transaction migration never
                      applied. They are now created before the types
                      and tables.
                    - Resolved ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE failing on a column
                      a row-level security policy or a trigger (WHEN, or
                      UPDATE OF) depends on, for a real type change as
                      for a collation change: PostgreSQL refuses it
                      while they exist, so the migration failed on every
                      run. Such policies and triggers, including another
                      table's policy whose subquery reads the column,
                      are now dropped ahead of the table changes and
                      created again from the target afterwards (with
                      --use-drop, from the dependencies the FROM dump
                      recorded).
                    - Resolved event triggers whose names need quotes
                      (mixed case, spaces) being quoted a second time:
                      the migration created a trigger literally named
                      "AuditTrg" with the quotes, and every drop,
                      enable/disable, owner change and comment missed
                      the real one, run after run.
                    - Resolved CREATE TABLE failing for a primary key
                      over a column whose quoted name holds ) or ,
                      ("Code (x)", "a,b"): the key's column list was
                      cut at the first ) and split at every ,. The
                      key's INCLUDE (...) and DEFERRABLE clauses, which
                      were left out so the next compare dropped and
                      re-added the key, are now kept too.
                    - Resolved dropping a table also dropping
                      unrelated foreign keys whose definition merely
                      contained its name (s.account in references
                      s.accounts(id), log in catalog_id). An unchanged
                      key was never added back; a changed one was
                      dropped twice, which failed with "does not exist"
                      and aborted a single-transaction migration. Only
                      a key that references the dropped table, or a
                      partitioned table the dropped partition belongs
                      to, is now dropped ahead of it, once, and the
                      ones the target keeps are added back. That
                      includes a key to the parent of a dropped
                      partition, which used to be lost.
                    - Resolved a pgc 1.0.x dump of a materialized view
                      named after a column-name or type/function-name
                      keyword (left, int, position, and from PostgreSQL
                      16 or 17 on json_object, json, merge_action)
                      failing to match a current dump of the same view,
                      which was then dropped and recreated with its
                      indexes, dependent views and grants. Such names
                      are now quoted as quote_ident does on the server
                      of the dump they are compared with.
                    - Resolved the routine language and a materialized
                      view's tablespace being written from the raw
                      catalog name; both are now quoted as quote_ident
                      does, and unchanged for the usual names.
                    - Resolved composite type changes never being
                      migrated. A type whose attributes changed got only
                      an "Alter script not implemented" comment, which
                      USE_COMMENTS=false strips, so the migration was
                      silently empty and the next compare looked
                      converged. Attributes are now dropped, retyped
                      (ALTER ATTRIBUTE ... SET DATA TYPE) and added in
                      place, with CASCADE when typed tables use the type,
                      after the views that read a changed column are
                      dropped. A renamed attribute is a drop and an add.
                      To reach the target order, attributes are dropped
                      and re-added only when nothing stores the type. A
                      retype PostgreSQL refuses because a column stores
                      the type is left out with a note. A drop with
                      CASCADE says, in a note and on stderr, that it also
                      drops what depends on the column. The dump records
                      the typed tables and storing columns of each
                      composite type; neither is hashed, and the query
                      that finds them stays fast with thousands of types.
                    - Resolved ALTER TABLE ... ADD / DROP COLUMN and
                      ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE being emitted for typed
                      tables, which PostgreSQL refuses. Their columns now
                      follow their type's ALTER TYPE ... CASCADE, and only
                      what the table adds itself (default, NOT NULL,
                      comment) is set on it. A column the type adds is
                      filled with its default before NOT NULL, as ADD
                      COLUMN ... DEFAULT would fill it. A typed table
                      whose type is outside the compared schemas gets a
                      note and a warning.
                    - Resolved sequence data type changes. ALTER SEQUENCE
                      had no AS clause, so serial -> bigserial (or an
                      integer -> bigint sequence or identity column)
                      failed on every run with MAXVALUE out of range, and
                      the reverse silently kept bigint. The new type is
                      now set first.
                    - Resolved owner changes of sequences linked to a
                      table failing on every run. pgc changed the owner of
                      a serial or identity sequence directly and first,
                      which PostgreSQL refuses for a sequence linked to a
                      table; the table's OWNER TO now carries it. A
                      sequence that moves to another table, or to none,
                      while its owner changes is unlinked first, and a
                      sequence that becomes OWNED BY a column while its
                      owner changes is linked after the tables.
                    - Resolved range types creating their multirange in
                      the migration role's search_path schema (usually
                      public) instead of the range's own. An unqualified
                      multirange_type_name was written, so every column,
                      routine or view using schema.name_multirange failed,
                      and two ranges of the same name in different schemas
                      collided. The name is now schema-qualified, and a
                      multirange an earlier pgc misplaced is moved with
                      ALTER TYPE ... SET SCHEMA.
                    - Resolved a routine with SET search_path TO NULL in
                      a PostgreSQL 19 TO being re-created by every
                      compare against an older FROM server, which can
                      only store the '' fallback. The routine is now
                      compared in that form, behind a note and a warning.
                    - Every type, enum or extension difference pgc leaves
                      in place now also prints a warning on stderr,
                      since USE_COMMENTS=false strips the comment that
                      said so. These are an enum value it cannot remove
                      or reorder, a domain base type, collation or kind
                      change, a type it cannot create, a composite retype
                      or reorder it cannot make, and an extension owner
                      change.

                Known limitations (add):
                    - A composite attribute retype, or a reorder, is not
                      migrated while columns or typed tables hold values
                      of the type. That includes columns the same
                      migration drops later; the next run makes the
                      change.
                    - ALTER TYPE ... DROP ATTRIBUTE ... CASCADE on a type
                      with typed tables also drops the objects depending
                      on the dropped column that pgc does not compare,
                      such as views in other schemas. PostgreSQL has no
                      way to reach the typed tables without it; pgc
                      warns.
                    - A domain's collation cannot be changed in place
                      (PostgreSQL has no ALTER DOMAIN ... COLLATE); pgc
                      warns.
Bullets for the v1.1.0 "Bug fixes" list, in the existing style:

                    - Resolved the enforceability of CHECK constraint
                      copies a table only inherits being ignored.
                      PostgreSQL 19 lets such a copy stay ENFORCED under
                      a NOT ENFORCED parent (18 reaches that state by
                      attaching a table), but pgc dumped only local
                      copies, so a parent's in-place NOT ENFORCED silently
                      stopped checking the child's rows. The dump now
                      records such a copy (an optional field, hashed only
                      when present). After every table alter, the
                      migration sets each copy that differs from the
                      target, parents first, with ALTER TABLE ... ALTER
                      CONSTRAINT ... [NOT] ENFORCED on PostgreSQL 19,
                      including a merged copy that a recursing NOT
                      ENFORCED reached. On older servers it writes a
                      comment and a warning instead.
                    - Resolved attaching a table as a partition. The
                      migration ran ATTACH PARTITION before the table's
                      own SET NOT NULL, ADD COLUMN and DROP COLUMN, so the
                      attach failed. It also dropped the table's copy of
                      the parent's CHECK after the attach had merged it
                      ("cannot drop inherited constraint"), and failed
                      the attach when the table lacked that CHECK or held
                      it with another definition. The attach now follows
                      the table's column changes. The parents' CHECKs are
                      added, re-added or given the target's
                      enforceability before it and never dropped after
                      it. The table, and its own partitions, are altered
                      after their new parents. The CHECK copies a DETACH
                      PARTITION leaves behind are dropped or kept in the
                      same migration.
                    - Resolved changes to a table's INHERITS parents never
                      being migrated. The table's copy of the parent's
                      CHECK was silently dropped, and the table never
                      joined its new parent. The migration now emits
                      ALTER TABLE ... NO INHERIT and INHERIT, with the
                      column changes and CHECKs the INHERIT needs. A CHECK
                      the target has the table only inherit is dropped by
                      its holders before the INHERIT and added back after
                      it, so the table's copy ends up inherited, as in the
                      target.
                    - Resolved a NOT NULL constraint (PostgreSQL 18 and
                      later) whose name no longer followed
                      {table}_{column}_not_null being dropped. That
                      happens after a column or table rename, on a
                      detached partition, or when a name moves to another
                      column. The column became nullable until the next
                      run, and on a primary-key or identity column the
                      drop failed on every run. NOT NULL constraints are
                      now paired by column. A name PostgreSQL generated is
                      left alone, and one the target chose, moved or
                      swapped is renamed, through a temporary name for a
                      swap. Each rename is guarded, so a partly applied
                      migration can run again.
                    - Resolved a foreign server whose foreign-data
                      wrapper changes being dropped with CASCADE, which
                      silently took its user mappings, every foreign
                      table on it with their privileges and comments, the
                      views over them, the server's own USAGE grants, and
                      a foreign table's place as a partition. The
                      migration now drops those foreign tables and views
                      and the server's user mappings itself, drops the
                      server without CASCADE, and creates it again with
                      its user mappings, grants and foreign tables,
                      column grants and partition attaches included,
                      followed by the views. On PostgreSQL 19 a server
                      that a SERVER subscription the migration keeps
                      still uses is left as it is, with a warning. A
                      foreign table the migration creates is now attached
                      to its partitioned table and given the target's
                      column grants, and a server it creates gets the
                      target's USAGE grants.
                    - Resolved a text search dictionary whose template
                      changes being dropped with CASCADE, which took the
                      configurations mapping it and the generated
                      tsvector columns and indexes built through them,
                      and needed three runs. The migration now creates the
                      new dictionary under a temporary name before the
                      tables, moves the configurations to it, drops the
                      old one without CASCADE and renames the new one,
                      all in one DO block. A drop that another object
                      blocks fails loudly and leaves nothing behind.

Known limitations (for the existing "Known limitations" list):

                    - A NOT NULL constraint that INHERIT needs the table
                      to have already stays local afterwards (PostgreSQL
                      cannot turn it into an inherited one), where a
                      table created under INHERITS only inherits it. It
                      matters only if the parent later drops its NOT
                      NULL.
                    - ATTACH PARTITION, DETACH PARTITION, INHERIT and NO
                      INHERIT are not guarded against re-running a partly
                      applied --output-for-production migration.
                    - With --grants-mode ignore, a foreign server that is
                      created again because its wrapper changes, and the
                      foreign tables on it, are not given their
                      privileges back (a warning says so).
                    - Resolved a dump whose schema pattern matches no
                      accessible schema holding none of the objects
                      that do not live in a schema: event triggers,
                      publications, subscriptions, foreign-data
                      wrappers, foreign servers, user mappings and the
                      extensions installed in public, which a dump of
                      any schema of the same database always holds.
                      Compared against such a dump, a migration dropped
                      every one of them, a subscription's replication
                      slot on the publisher included, even when the
                      target database held them too; from such a dump,
                      the first deployment into a database that already
                      held some created them again and failed. Such a
                      dump now reads them like any other. The clear
                      command still reads nothing for a pattern that
                      matches no schema, so its script drops nothing.
                    - Resolved an identity column's sequence going
                      unmigrated after the column was renamed.
                      PostgreSQL keeps the sequence's old name when its
                      column is renamed, so the source and target dumps
                      held it under two different names and any change
                      to it - options, privileges - was never compared.
                      The sequence is now renamed to its target name
                      first and then compared as usual.
                    - Resolved a composite type's attribute type change
                      failing when a policy or trigger depends on the
                      corresponding column of a table typed by it: ALTER
                      TYPE ... ALTER ATTRIBUTE ... CASCADE runs before
                      the tables are changed, and PostgreSQL refuses it
                      while such a dependent exists. Those policies and
                      triggers are now dropped just before the type is
                      altered and created again afterwards.
                    - Resolved a composite type's attribute retype
                      (ALTER TYPE ... ALTER ATTRIBUTE ... TYPE ...
                      CASCADE) failing when what blocks it sat on a
                      partition or INHERITS descendant of a typed table,
                      on a typed table the migration drops or recreates
                      later, or on another table whose policy reads the
                      column: the statement reaches all of them, but only
                      the kept typed tables' own policies and triggers
                      were dropped ahead of it. They are now all dropped
                      just before the composite section and created from
                      the target once, after every table alter (nothing
                      on a dropped table; a recreated one brings its own
                      back). A policy reading a recreated typed table was
                      dropped and never created again. Attributes whose
                      names need quoting (mixed case, reserved words) are
                      matched to their typed tables' columns as well.
                    - Resolved the same retype failing with "cannot alter
                      type of a column used by a generated column" when an
                      inheritance descendant of a typed table has a
                      generated column reading the retyped column: the
                      column was only dropped in the table phase, after
                      the type. It is now dropped (or loses its
                      expression) before the type's ALTER, after the
                      policies, triggers and foreign keys on it, and added
                      back in its table's alter. On a descendant the
                      migration drops, a copy inherited from an ancestor
                      that is handled the same way is left to that
                      ancestor.
                    - Resolved a change of a composite type attribute's
                      collation never being migrated, and a new composite
                      type losing an attribute's COLLATE. The dump now
                      records an attribute's own collation (left out of
                      the dump and of the hash when it is the data type's
                      default, so every other type keeps its hash), CREATE
                      TYPE and ADD ATTRIBUTE write it, and a change is an
                      ALTER ATTRIBUTE ... SET DATA TYPE ... COLLATE ...
                      CASCADE. A FROM dump written by pgc 1.0.x takes the
                      target's collations, as it recorded none.
                    - Resolved the policies, triggers, views, rules,
                      SQL-standard routine bodies and publication column
                      lists or row filters over a typed table's column
                      being dropped and created again on every run when
                      the column differs but its type does not retype it
                      (the type is outside the compared schemas, or a
                      column stores it): the migration counted the column
                      as retyped by the table, which ALTER TABLE cannot
                      do. A table that leaves the typed table's family
                      still retypes its own column, with what blocks it
                      out of the way.
                    - Resolved a policy that reads both a column one table
                      drops and re-adds and a column another table retypes
                      in place being created again right after the first
                      table's alter, where it blocked the second table's
                      ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE. It is now created once, after
                      every table alter.
                    - Resolved a new or recreated typed table being
                      created as CREATE TABLE ... OF type followed by full
                      column definitions, a syntax error on every version
                      (a partitioned typed table whose partition key
                      changed was dropped and never created again). It is
                      now created as CREATE TABLE ... OF type with only
                      what it adds: column WITH OPTIONS defaults and NOT
                      NULL constraints and the primary key, or no list at
                      all.
                    - Resolved CREATE SUBSCRIPTION contacting the
                      publisher for a subscription only TO has. A
                      disabled subscription, or one without a
                      replication slot, was written WITH (enabled =
                      false), so PostgreSQL connected to its publisher:
                      the migration failed when the publisher was
                      unreachable, and on a reachable one it created a
                      replication slot that the disabled subscription
                      never consumed and that kept retaining WAL there.
                      The dump now records the slot
                      (pg_subscription.subslotname), and such a
                      subscription is created WITH (connect = false,
                      slot_name = NONE or '...', enabled = false), as
                      pg_dump writes it; only an enabled subscription is
                      still created connected, naming its slot when the
                      slot is not named after the subscription. A slot
                      that differs between FROM and TO, which no compare
                      could see before, becomes ALTER SUBSCRIPTION ...
                      SET (slot_name = ...), with an enabled
                      subscription disabled first for slot_name = NONE;
                      the statement neither drops the old slot nor
                      creates the new one on the publisher, which a
                      comment in the script says. A subscription on a
                      slot named after it keeps its pgc 1.0.x hash, and
                      a pgc 1.0.x dump, which lacks the slot, reads as
                      one.
                    - Resolved migrations that wrote a replication slot
                      name PostgreSQL refuses as slot_name. CREATE
                      SUBSCRIPTION ... WITH (connect = false) records
                      the subscription's own name as its slot unchecked,
                      so a slot can be named "My Sub", x"y or none. SET
                      (slot_name = 'x"y') then failed on every run, and
                      slot_name = 'none' silently cleared the slot. Such
                      a slot name, anything but 1 to 63 lower-case
                      letters, digits and underscores, none, and
                      pg_conflict_detection on PostgreSQL 19, is now
                      left out, with a comment in the script and a
                      warning on stderr, so the migration applies and
                      the next compare is empty. An enabled subscription
                      whose own name is no valid slot name is created
                      with connect = false, since the publisher would
                      refuse to create that slot.

                Internals:
                    - The new publication fields are left out of the dump
                      at their default and folded into the hash only when
                      set, so dumps written by pgc 1.0.x load unchanged
                      and publications that use none of the new
                      properties keep their 1.0.x hashes and SQL.

                Tests:
                    - PostgreSQL 19 fixtures for SERVER subscriptions,
                      an owner change together with a server switch,
                      the three subscription options and FDW CONNECTION
                      functions, all built from subscriptions created
                      with connect = false present on both sides; unit
                      tests for both query versions, the ALTER
                      orderings, the drop ordering, the single
                      transaction warning and the hash and dump format
                      compatibility.
                    - Added fixtures for changed, PARALLEL, SORTOP and
                      unbounded-state aggregates, routine and aggregate
                      kind changes, the privileges of recreated
                      routines, aggregate SUPPORT and the list-parameter
                      SET forms, a SplitGUCList-equivalent splitter and
                      numeric-constant scanner with their own tests, and
                      hash pins showing that only aggregates with a
                      negative state size hash differently from 1.0.x.
                    - `cargo test` now runs 2363 tests, up from 1106 in
                      v1.0.27, and 12 more that need a live server stay
                      `#[ignore]`d (run them with `cargo test --
                      --ignored` and the standard `PG*` environment
                      variables). Every fixture case the CI round trip
                      covers is also checked directly against the
                      catalogs after the migration, by the
                      `.github/scripts/assert-*.sh` steps.

                Tooling:
                    - CI tests PostgreSQL 19. The integration matrix
                      gained a 19 leg that resolves its image at run
                      time: the GA postgres:19 tag once it is published,
                      the newest 19rcN or 19betaN tag until then, so the
                      job follows the release without an edit.
                    - CI checks the migrated catalogs directly. After
                      round 1 is applied, .github/scripts/assert-*.sh
                      compare the relevant catalogs of the migrated
                      database with the target and spot-check that the
                      fixture values arrived. The round-2 compare cannot
                      see a property pgc loses on both sides, since both
                      dumps then agree; these steps can. Each is guarded
                      by server_version_num where the catalogs differ
                      between majors.

                Documentation:
                    - README gained a Supported PostgreSQL versions
                      section: 14 through 19, how the FROM server's
                      version selects the DDL pgc emits, and the caveats
                      of comparing across majors, among them PostgreSQL
                      19's change of the default GiST operator class for
                      inet and cidr.
                    - data/test/README.md documents every new fixture
                      case, and fills the missing section 45.

                Known limitations:
                    - A partition or INHERITS child that the target
                      takes out of its family while keeping the old
                      column type gets the new type from the ancestor's
                      recursing retype, for ordinary and typed tables
                      alike; the next run retypes it back.
                    - An INHERITS descendant's generated column over a
                      typed column that no statement retypes is dropped
                      and re-added on every run.
                    - pgc 1.0.x cannot read a dump written by this
                      version of a schema that has extended statistics,
                      or a trigger declared with UPDATE OF columns or
                      with a WHEN clause: the dump now records which
                      columns these objects depend on, in a form 1.0.x
                      does not know. Dumps written by 1.0.x still load,
                      but they carry neither the deparsed statistics ON
                      list nor those dependencies, so statistics and
                      triggers taken along with a rewritten column are
                      only restored once the source database is dumped
                      again with this version.
                    - pgc 1.0.x could not read statistics targets from
                      PostgreSQL 17 and later. Comparing a source dump
                      written by 1.0.x of such a server with a target
                      dump written by this version emits one ALTER
                      STATISTICS ... SET STATISTICS per statistics
                      object with an explicit target.

2026-09-06      v1.0.27

                Fixes four migrations that did not do what they said -
                the routine ordering bug behind issue #240, with one
                opt-in flag for it, the schema-moved extension of
                issue #241, the materialized view rebuilt on an
                unrelated column change of issue #242, and the
                identity column dropped in the wrong order of issue
                #243 - and one class of password that could not be
                used to connect at all (issue #244). Beyond those
                this release reorganises the test suite, adds a
                library target and integration tests, and documents
                the crate. The dump gains one optional field, which
                older dumps may omit, and the generated SQL is
                unchanged for any schema none of the four migration
                bugs touched.

                Bug fixes:
                    - Resolved issue #244 (a password containing
                      characters that are reserved in a URL could not be
                      used, and one kind of password was silently
                      changed). Connection details were interpolated
                      into a postgres://user:password@host:port/database
                      string and handed back to sqlx to parse, so every
                      reserved character in a credential was read as URL
                      syntax rather than as data. A password containing
                      #, / or ? ended the authority section early and
                      the parser eventually read the port out of
                      something that was not a number, reporting invalid
                      port number - naming the one part of the
                      connection that was never wrong, before any socket
                      was opened. Worse and quieter, a password
                      containing a valid percent-escape was decoded:
                      pass%41word was sent as passAword, so the wrong
                      password was accepted and the real one could not
                      be used at all. The connection now goes through
                      PgConnectOptions with the fields set individually,
                      which sqlx passes to the wire protocol unaltered,
                      and a port that is not a number is reported as a
                      port instead of as a failed connection. The masked
                      connection string in log output is unchanged.
                    - Resolved issue #243 (a column that stopped being
                      an identity column produced a migration PostgreSQL
                      refused to run). One column's ALTER statements
                      were emitted in a fixed source order - type,
                      default, nullability, identity - so the identity
                      came off last. PostgreSQL holds an identity column
                      NOT NULL, forbids it a default and restricts it to
                      an integer type, and enforces each of those at the
                      moment of the ALTER, so every statement that
                      relaxed one of them ran while the column was still
                      an identity column and was rejected. The reported
                      case was DROP NOT NULL; SET DEFAULT and a move to
                      a non-integer type failed the same way and are
                      fixed with it. The identity drop now leads the
                      column's statements. Adding an identity needs the
                      opposite order and already had it, so the add side
                      is untouched: a column must already be NOT NULL
                      and have no default before ADD GENERATED AS
                      IDENTITY will take.
                    - Resolved issue #242 (a materialized view was
                      dropped and fully rebuilt whenever any column of
                      any table it reads changed, even a column it never
                      reads). The set of views that have to be dropped
                      before the table DDL runs was decided at table
                      granularity: a view over a table whose hash
                      differed at all went first. PostgreSQL's own rule
                      is much narrower. It refuses to drop or retype a
                      column a view depends on, and refuses nothing
                      else, so adding a column is permitted with every
                      view in place. A metadata-only ADD COLUMN
                      therefore turned into a DROP MATERIALIZED VIEW, a
                      full rebuild of its contents and a rebuild of each
                      of its indexes, with the data missing until the
                      refresh finished. Regular views over the same
                      table were dropped and recreated for the same
                      reason. The dump now records which columns each
                      view actually reads, taken from pg_depend for the
                      view's _RETURN rule, and only a change to one of
                      those columns drags the view along. A table that
                      is dropped, or dropped and recreated, still takes
                      every view over it. Dumps written before this
                      field exists keep the old table-level answer.
                    - Resolved issue #241 (an extension moved between
                      schemas was deleted instead of relocated).
                      Extensions were matched between the two dumps on
                      (schema, name), but an extension's name is its
                      identity: pg_extension has a unique index on
                      extname, and the schema only records where it was
                      installed. A move therefore paired with nothing
                      and was emitted as two unrelated events, a create
                      in the new schema and a drop in the old. That pair
                      destroys the extension without reporting anything:
                      create extension if not exists is a no-op once the
                      name is taken, so the drop that follows removes
                      the only copy, the migration applies cleanly, and
                      every function the extension provided is gone.
                      Extensions are now matched by name, which routes a
                      move to the alter extension ... set schema branch
                      that Extension already had and that nothing could
                      reach. Caveat: an extension that PostgreSQL marks
                      non-relocatable cannot be moved by ALTER at all,
                      so that case now fails loudly on replay rather
                      than deleting anything.
                    - Resolved issue #240 (a routine named in a comment
                      or a string literal was read as a call, and the
                      migration then created routines before the
                      routines they call). PostgreSQL records no
                      dependency for a call inside a function body, so
                      pgc infers routine creation order by scanning each
                      body's text for qualified schema.name references.
                      The scan could not tell a call from a name written
                      in prose or quoted inside dynamic SQL, and neither
                      of those is resolved when the routine is created.
                      Each such name added a graph edge that was not
                      real; one of them pointing back along a real edge
                      closed a cycle, and a cycle cannot be
                      topologically sorted, so the routines in it and
                      everything blocked behind them fell back to plain
                      name order. A schema where one routine's comment
                      mentioned its caller was enough to emit a
                      migration that failed on function ... does not
                      exist. Comments and string literal bodies, both
                      single-quoted and dollar-quoted, are now blanked
                      before the scan; double-quoted identifiers are
                      kept, because a quoted identifier is a reference.
                      This covers the same scan wherever it decides an
                      order: routine creates and drops, view drops, and
                      the combined routine and view pass.

                Features:
                    - New --guard-sql-routine-bodies flag (config key
                      GUARD_SQL_ROUTINE_BODIES), default false. When
                      set, the migration opens with set
                      check_function_bodies = false, so PostgreSQL does
                      not resolve the names inside a routine body at
                      CREATE time and a routine is created even if its
                      callee is not there yet. A defensive net for the
                      whole class of ordering problem above, since a
                      text scan can only ever approximate what a body
                      calls. The cost, and the reason it is opt-in, is
                      that a genuine mistake in a routine body is no
                      longer caught while the migration runs. The
                      statement is SET rather than SET LOCAL so it also
                      covers a production script's post-commit section;
                      a migration with nothing to do withdraws it, so an
                      empty diff is unchanged.
                    - A dependency cycle among routines or views is now
                      named on stderr instead of passing silently. The
                      report is narrowed to the objects that actually
                      form the cycle rather than everything Kahn's sort
                      was left holding, which also includes whatever was
                      merely blocked behind it. A genuine cycle is
                      possible: mutually recursive routines really do
                      depend on each other, and no ordering satisfies
                      them.

                Internals:
                    - Added a library target (`app/src/lib.rs`)
                      exporting the four existing modules. `main.rs`
                      is now a thin binary over it and holds only CLI
                      parsing and command dispatch. A binary-only
                      crate cannot have integration tests or run
                      doctests, so this is what the two sections below
                      are built on. The binary's behaviour, its flags
                      and its output are unaffected.

                Tests:
                    - Unit tests moved out of the source directories:
                      `src/<module>/<name>_tests.rs` is now
                      `src/<module>/tests/<name>.rs`. They remain
                      `#[cfg(test)] #[path = ...] mod tests;` children
                      of the module they cover, so they still reach
                      its private items; only the file layout changed.
                      `src/dump/` no longer interleaves 27 test files
                      with the 28 sources beside them.
                    - `comparer/core_tests.rs`, at 11,772 lines the
                      largest file in the project, was split by
                      concern into fifteen files under
                      `src/comparer/tests/core/` — grants, views,
                      persistence, routines, sequences, tables and so
                      on — with the fixtures used by more than one of
                      them in `helpers.rs`. The before and after test
                      name sets are identical. Note that these
                      submodules need explicit `#[path]` attributes:
                      their parent is itself loaded through `#[path]`,
                      so rustc resolves children against
                      `src/comparer/tests/` rather than the `core/`
                      subdirectory, and a bare `mod production;`
                      silently binds to the unrelated
                      `tests/production.rs`.
                    - New integration suite in `app/tests/`, thirty
                      tests over the public API only, covering ground
                      the in-memory unit tests cannot reach: the zip
                      dump file round-trip and the `#[serde(default)]`
                      contract that keeps dumps from older versions
                      readable; the full dump → file → dump → compare
                      → script path the `compare` command takes,
                      including that a schema compared against itself
                      emits no DDL; `Config::load` against real files,
                      among them the shipped `data/pgc.conf`; and the
                      drop ordering of the `clear` command. Three
                      further tests exercise a live server and are
                      `#[ignore]`d by default — run them with
                      `cargo test -- --ignored` and the standard `PG*`
                      environment variables.
                    - `cargo test` now runs 1106 tests, up from 1062.

                Documentation:
                    - Module-level documentation on all 36 modules.
                      `dump/mod.rs` describes the
                      hash / get_script / get_drop_script /
                      get_alter_script shape that nearly every object
                      module repeats, and what adding a new object
                      kind requires, so the individual module headers
                      only carry what is specific to that kind.
                    - 245 comments on public items that were written
                      with `//`, and therefore invisible to rustdoc,
                      promoted to `///`. Trailing comments moved above
                      the field they describe.
                    - Fourteen doctests on the primary public API.
                      They compile and run under `cargo test`, so the
                      examples cannot drift from the code.
                    - Fixed eight broken intra-doc links that had been
                      rendering as plain text. Four were only visible
                      with `--document-private-items`.

                Tooling:
                    - CI gained a docs step running
                      `cargo doc --no-deps --lib
                      --document-private-items` under
                      `RUSTDOCFLAGS="-D warnings"`, so an unresolved
                      link fails the build instead of degrading
                      silently. Private items are included because pgc
                      ships as a binary: most of what a contributor
                      reads is private, and a broken link there is
                      just as wrong.

2026-07-28      v1.0.26

                Bug fixes:
                    - Resolved issue #235 (materialized view
                      recreation did not restore its indexes). A
                      materialized view can be indexed, but the index
                      rows PostgreSQL reports for one were only ever
                      distributed into the dump's table list, which
                      holds ordinary and partitioned tables alone, so
                      they matched nothing and never reached the
                      dump. Nothing re-emitted them either: a
                      materialized view is always dropped and
                      recreated when its definition changes, and DROP
                      MATERIALIZED VIEW takes the indexes with it, so
                      the migration silently left the view unindexed.
                      Because neither dump carried the indexes the
                      second diff was empty, and the loss went
                      unreported. The dump now captures the indexes
                      of every materialized view alongside the view
                      itself. A view that is recreated has them
                      rebuilt with it, and one whose definition did
                      not change has its index set reconciled in
                      place (created, dropped, redefined or
                      re-commented) so that adding an index no longer
                      forces a full rebuild of the view's contents.
                      A view restored after DROP FUNCTION ... CASCADE
                      gets the same treatment, and with
                      --output-for-production the builds and drops
                      run CONCURRENTLY after the transaction commits,
                      exactly as a table's do. Dumps written by older
                      versions carry no index data; they stay
                      readable and simply report no indexes.

2026-07-17      v1.0.25

                Bug fixes:
                    - Resolved issue #229 (temporary session schemas
                      included when dumping with --scheme %). A broad
                      scheme pattern picked up pg_toast and, whenever
                      any session held temporary objects at dump time,
                      the per-session pg_temp_N / pg_toast_temp_N
                      schemas together with their temporary tables.
                      The generated create schema pg_temp_N then
                      failed with "unacceptable schema name: the
                      prefix pg_ is reserved for system schemas". The
                      schema-resolution query now excludes every
                      pg_-prefixed schema, which propagates to all
                      dump, compare and clear paths since they filter
                      by the resolved schema list. PostgreSQL reserves
                      the pg_ prefix, so no user schema can match the
                      filter and nothing dumpable is lost.
                    - Resolved issue #227 (CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW
                      generated when the column order changes, causing
                      migration failure). PostgreSQL accepts CREATE OR
                      REPLACE VIEW only when every existing column
                      keeps its name, type and collation at the same
                      position and new columns are appended at the
                      end; inserting a column in the middle,
                      reordering, renaming, retyping or dropping one
                      fails with "cannot change name of view column".
                      The dump now captures each regular view's output
                      columns (name, type including typmod, and
                      collation), and a changed view whose old column
                      list is not an exact prefix of the new one is
                      dropped and recreated instead of replaced.
                      Because that DROP VIEW runs without CASCADE,
                      views reading a dropped view are pulled into the
                      drop set transitively, dropped first in
                      dependency order and recreated afterwards — this
                      also covers dependents of changed materialized
                      views, whose drops could previously fail the
                      same way. Compatible changes (appending columns
                      at the end, or body-only changes) still use
                      CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW, and dumps written by
                      older pgc versions carry no column data, so they
                      keep the historical behavior.
                    - Resolved issue #226 (non-idempotent PostgreSQL
                      expression normalization caused an infinite diff
                      loop). PostgreSQL deparses `col IN ('FOO','BAR')`
                      as an array-level cast
                      `(ARRAY[...])::text[]` on first render, then as
                      an element-level cast `ARRAY[(...)::text, ...]`
                      once that form is re-parsed. Both are equivalent
                      but differ textually, and pgc compares the
                      deparsed definition text, so it kept re-emitting
                      DROP+CREATE for any materialized view, partial
                      index, generated column, or check constraint
                      whose expression carried an IN-list. Definitions
                      are now canonicalized before hashing and
                      comparison: the array-level cast is rewritten
                      into PostgreSQL's element-level fixed point (and
                      the paren-free CHECK-constraint form keeps
                      collapsing as before), so equivalent renderings
                      compare equal and the second diff is empty. The
                      canonicalizer is literal- and quoted-identifier
                      aware, so string contents and case-sensitive
                      identifiers are never altered.
                    - Resolved issue #223 (CREATE INDEX on a
                      partitioned table used ON ONLY without attaching
                      the partition indexes, leaving the index
                      invalid). pg_get_indexdef renders a partitioned
                      parent's index with ON ONLY, which creates only
                      the metadata index on the parent and leaves it
                      indisvalid=false until every partition's index is
                      built and attached. The default output emitted
                      that ON ONLY form verbatim with no attach step,
                      so the resulting index was permanently invalid
                      and no partition indexes were created. The
                      non-production output now emits a plain CREATE
                      INDEX ... ON, so PostgreSQL builds and attaches
                      the partition indexes itself and the parent index
                      is valid immediately. Production output
                      (OUTPUT_FOR_PRODUCTION=true) keeps emitting ON
                      ONLY followed by the per-partition CREATE INDEX
                      CONCURRENTLY and ATTACH PARTITION sequence, and
                      indexes recreated after a DROP FUNCTION ...
                      CASCADE now go through that same partition-aware
                      concurrent sequence too — previously that path
                      re-emitted the raw definition, leaving the
                      recreated parent index permanently invalid in
                      every mode.
                    - Resolved issue #218 (spurious DROP CONSTRAINT
                      and ADD CONSTRAINT for inline NOT NULL). A
                      column's inline NOT NULL is surfaced by
                      PostgreSQL 18 in pg_constraint under the
                      auto-generated name {table}_{column}_not_null,
                      while PostgreSQL 14–17 keep it only in
                      pg_attribute.attnotnull. When a dump taken from
                      PG18 drove a diff, the constraint pass emitted
                      DROP CONSTRAINT for a dropped NOT NULL column and
                      ADD CONSTRAINT for a column that gained NOT NULL,
                      on top of the correct column-level SET/DROP NOT
                      NULL. The DROP fails on PG14–17 (the named
                      constraint does not exist there); the ADD is
                      PG18-only syntax and leaves a named constraint
                      the source never declared, so the next compare
                      keeps trying to drop it. NOT NULL constraints
                      whose name matches PostgreSQL's auto-generated
                      {table}_{column}_not_null pattern (optionally
                      with a numeric collision suffix) are now left
                      entirely to the column-level NOT NULL handling
                      and are never emitted as ADD or DROP CONSTRAINT.
                      A NOT NULL constraint whose name differs from
                      that pattern is treated as an explicit constraint
                      and is unaffected; a user-chosen name that
                      happens to match the pattern is indistinguishable
                      from an auto-generated one and is handled the
                      same way (the column still ends up NOT NULL).
                    - Resolved issue #217 (SET configuration values
                      wrapped in single quotes, causing an infinite
                      diff loop). CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION/PROCEDURE
                      quoted every SET value as a string literal,
                      including list-valued GUCs such as search_path
                      and temp_tablespaces. Their proconfig value is a
                      bare comma-separated list, so wrapping it turned
                      `t, pg_temp` into a single schema literally named
                      "t, pg_temp"; the applied routine then re-stored
                      differently from the source and every subsequent
                      compare re-emitted the routine forever. Values of
                      GUC_LIST_QUOTE parameters are now emitted
                      verbatim, the way pg_get_functiondef does, while
                      scalar GUCs stay quoted as literals — which also
                      fixes the mirror case of a scalar value that
                      happens to contain a double quote, previously
                      emitted unquoted and invalid.
                    - Resolved issue #216 (reloptions missing from
                      partitions when a table is recreated as
                      partitioned). Turning a regular table into a
                      partitioned one goes through DROP + CREATE, and
                      the partitions are then built with CREATE TABLE
                      ... PARTITION OF. That statement was emitted
                      without a WITH (...) clause, so every partition
                      came back with no storage parameters at all and
                      the migration only reached the target schema on
                      a second pass — the first diff applied, and
                      comparing again still asked for the reloptions.
                      PARTITION OF accepts WITH (...) like any other
                      CREATE TABLE, and the clause is now emitted
                      there, between PARTITION BY and TABLESPACE as
                      the grammar requires. The opposite direction,
                      partitioned to regular, already carried its
                      reloptions through the plain CREATE TABLE.
                    - Resolved issue #220 (CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW in
                      generated diff is missing WITH NO DATA clause). A
                      materialized view created WITH NO DATA is left
                      unpopulated, but that is recorded only in
                      pg_class.relispopulated — the stored definition
                      is just the SELECT — so a diff rebuilt from the
                      definition dropped the clause and applying it ran
                      the query and filled the view the clause exists
                      to keep empty. The dump now reads ispopulated
                      and the generated CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW
                      carries WITH NO DATA again. Whether a view holds
                      data is state rather than schema (REFRESH ...
                      WITH NO DATA flips it either way), so it stays
                      out of the view hash: refreshing a materialized
                      view still does not make compare want to drop and
                      recreate it. Dumps written by earlier versions
                      read back as populated.
                    - Trailing view clauses are no longer emitted after
                      the statement they belong to. PostgreSQL renders
                      a view definition with its terminating semicolon,
                      and both WITH NO DATA and WITH CHECK OPTION were
                      appended after it, so the clause parsed as a
                      separate statement. WITH CHECK OPTION therefore
                      failed with a syntax error and the view was
                      created without the option — silently, since the
                      CREATE itself succeeded. Clauses are now written
                      inside the statement, and the statement is
                      terminated once, whether or not the definition
                      arrived with a semicolon of its own.
                    - Resolved issue #219 (views that reference only
                      functions are silently excluded from dump).
                      Regular views were read with an inner join
                      against information_schema.view_table_usage,
                      which holds no rows for a view whose body never
                      reads a table, so a view built solely on a
                      function call was dropped from the dump and
                      compare could not see any change to it. That
                      view is now dumped, and the relations it
                      references are resolved from the view's _RETURN
                      rewrite rule via pg_rewrite / pg_depend instead.
                      view_table_usage is also
                      privilege-filtered to tables owned by a
                      currently enabled role, so the same join
                      dropped any view whose underlying table
                      belonged to another role; that case is fixed
                      as well, which brings the view path in line
                      with the privilege-independent dump contract
                      the other introspection queries already honour.
                    - table_relation for materialized views is no
                      longer always empty. It was read from pg_depend
                      rows anchored on the materialized view's
                      pg_class OID, but a view's dependencies on the
                      relations it reads are recorded against its
                      _RETURN rewrite rule, so the lookup never
                      matched anything. Materialized views therefore
                      carried no dependency information at all:
                      compare could not tell that a materialized view
                      had to be dropped before an altered table it
                      reads, and clear emitted materialized-view
                      drops without ordering them against the tables
                      they depend on.

2026-06-11      v1.0.24

                Bug fixes:
                    - pg_depend - additional filter by classid and
                      objsubid to prevent objects from being excluded.
                      Every not exists(...) guard that filters
                      extension-owned objects was missing the classid
                      (catalog OID) and objsubid = 0 (whole-object)
                      conditions, so a stale pg_depend row in a
                      different catalog whose objid happened to collide
                      with a user-created object's OID caused that
                      object to be omitted from the dump.

2026-06-03      v1.0.23

                Features:
                    - Production-friendly output for the compare
                      command, enabled with --output-for-production
                      (or OUTPUT_FOR_PRODUCTION=true in the config
                      file). When on, the generated migration is
                      shaped to minimise locking on a live
                      database: indexes are built with CREATE INDEX
                      CONCURRENTLY (the UNIQUE keyword is
                      preserved); indexes on partitioned parents
                      use CREATE INDEX ... ON ONLY {parent} inside
                      the transaction, then CREATE INDEX
                      CONCURRENTLY on each partition followed by
                      ALTER INDEX ... ATTACH PARTITION afterwards
                      (falling back to a single non-concurrent
                      CREATE INDEX on the parent, with an
                      explanatory comment, when the layout cannot
                      be expanded safely); indexes are dropped with
                      DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY (kept non-concurrent
                      for indexes on partitioned tables, where
                      concurrent drop is illegal); and new foreign
                      keys are added NOT VALID inside the
                      transaction and validated with a separate
                      VALIDATE CONSTRAINT afterwards. Every
                      statement that cannot run inside a
                      transaction block (CREATE/DROP INDEX
                      CONCURRENTLY, VALIDATE CONSTRAINT, ALTER
                      INDEX ... ATTACH PARTITION) is emitted in a
                      clearly marked Production post-commit section
                      after commit. Defaults to false; when off,
                      the output is byte-for-byte identical to
                      previous behaviour.
                    - Added the OUTPUT_FOR_PRODUCTION key to the
                      sample data/pgc.conf and documented the new
                      flag, configuration key, and behaviour in
                      README.md.
                    - Production output is now re-runnable: every
                      DDL form PostgreSQL supports is emitted defensively
                      with an idempotency guard where available, so a
                      partially-applied migration can be replayed without failing.
                      CREATE [UNLOGGED] TABLE, CREATE [UNLOGGED]
                      SEQUENCE, CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW and CREATE
                      [UNIQUE] INDEX [CONCURRENTLY] gain IF NOT
                      EXISTS; CREATE VIEW becomes CREATE OR REPLACE
                      VIEW; ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN gains IF NOT
                      EXISTS; and ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN /
                      DROP CONSTRAINT gain IF EXISTS. CREATE TYPE
                      and ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT are left
                      as-is (PostgreSQL has no idempotency guard for
                      them). The rewrite is literal-, comment- and
                      dollar-quote-aware, so quoted text, commented
                      drops and function bodies are untouched, and
                      it only applies when
                      --output-for-production is on.

2026-05-26      v1.0.22

                Bug fixes:
                    - Rewrote `Table::build_columns_query` to read
                      column metadata directly from `pg_catalog`
                      (`pg_attribute`, `pg_attrdef`, `pg_depend`,
                      `pg_sequence`, `pg_collation`, `pg_rewrite`)
                      instead of from `information_schema.columns`
                      and `information_schema.view_column_usage`.
                      Those standard views are privilege-filtered
                      via `pg_has_role(...) OR
                      has_column_privilege(...)`, so a dump taken by
                      a role that was not the table owner and did
                      not have `SELECT` on every column silently
                      produced an empty / truncated column list —
                      strictly worse than a panic, because the
                      downstream comparer then emitted destructive
                      `ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN` deltas against a
                      schema that still had every column in place.
                      The new query has no privilege filter, so the
                      dump is faithful regardless of which role
                      runs `pgc`. Typmod-derived metrics still go
                      through `information_schema._pg_*` helpers
                      (plain SQL, not privilege-filtered). Fields
                      that are not consumed by SQL emission
                      (`is_updatable`, `character_set_*`, `scope_*`,
                      `maximum_cardinality`, `interval_precision`,
                      `is_self_referencing`, `dtd_identifier`) are
                      returned as constants and explicitly
                      documented as safe at the top of
                      `build_columns_query`.
                    - Fixed false positive diffs for auto-named NOT
                      NULL constraints. PostgreSQL 17+ records every
                      `NOT NULL` column constraint as a named
                      `pg_constraint` row using the pattern
                      `{table}_{col}_not_null`. When two tables in
                      the same schema collide on the base name
                      (e.g. `processing_result.status_id` and
                      `processing_result_status.id` both producing
                      `processing_result_status_id_not_null`), PG
                      appends a numeric collision suffix to one of
                      them. Which side receives the suffix depends
                      on creation order, so two semantically
                      identical databases populated by migrations
                      that ran in different orders ended up with
                      the suffix attached to different tables, and
                      the comparer emitted a destructive DROP/ADD
                      pair on every dump. The fix detects
                      auto-generated NOT NULL names (handling the
                      63-byte identifier truncation and the
                      re-clipping that PG performs when appending
                      the suffix would otherwise overflow), and
                      matches such constraints by column instead of
                      name.

                Behavioural verification:
                    - Unit-test coverage in
                      `app/src/dump/table_tests.rs` was extended
                      with `fetch_columns_query_pins_load_bearing_field_semantics`,
                      which structurally pins the pg_catalog
                      expressions for every load-bearing field
                      (identity_*, is_identity, is_generated,
                      generation_expression, is_nullable,
                      column_default, formatted_data_type CASE
                      arms, related_views path, and the outer
                      relkind filter). The previous test only
                      asserted the query did not reference
                      `information_schema.columns`.
                    - Manual verification path: dump the project's
                      own `data/test/schema_a.sql` against PG14
                      through PG18 with the new query, then `diff`
                      the serialized `Dump.tables[*].columns`
                      against a dump produced by the v1.0.21
                      query. Expected delta: zero, except for
                      `dtd_identifier` (now `None` everywhere — see
                      hardcoded-fields note above; `dtd_identifier`
                      does not drive any SQL emission so the
                      change is invisible at the migration level).
                      `is_updatable` may also differ on views: the
                      old query computed it from view privileges,
                      the new query hardcodes `'YES'`. Same caveat
                      — `is_updatable` is not consumed downstream.

                Caveats:
                    - Composite types (`relkind = 'c'`) are
                      intentionally excluded from the column query;
                      composite attributes are dumped via a
                      separate path in `core.rs` and would otherwise
                      double-emit.
                    - PG18 virtual generated columns
                      (`attgenerated = 'v'`) are correctly handled:
                      the `generation_expression` branch fires on
                      `attgenerated <> ''`, which covers both
                      stored (`'s'`) and virtual (`'v'`); the raw
                      `attgenerated` character survives as a
                      separate column so virtual vs stored stays
                      differentiable (see PR #185).
                    - A user-chosen constraint name that happens to
                      follow the exact
                      `{table}_{col}_not_null[_digits]` pattern is
                      now treated as auto-generated: `CREATE TABLE`
                      will emit a plain `NOT NULL` on the column
                      rather than `CONSTRAINT <name> NOT NULL`, and
                      a rename diff against another `..._not_null`
                      variant on the same column is suppressed. In
                      practice users either pick distinctive names
                      (e.g. `..._not_null_v2`, which has a non-digit
                      tail and is correctly rejected) or do not name
                      the constraint at all, so this misclassification
                      should be extremely rare. If you specifically
                      need to preserve a custom name that matches the
                      pattern, rename the constraint to something
                      that does not end in `_not_null` or
                      `_not_null<digits>`.

                Enhancements:
                    - Resolved issue #203 (deduplicate view definition
                      NULL-check in dump core). The NULL-check for the
                      `view_definition` column was previously repeated
                      verbatim in the regular-view and materialized-view
                      arms of `Dump::fetch_views_standalone`. Both arms
                      now route through a new
                      `Dump::require_view_definition` private helper
                      that returns the unwrapped definition or an
                      `Error::other` naming the affected
                      `schema.name` and the privilege hint. The helper
                      takes `Option<String>` (not a `PgRow`) so it is
                      independently unit-testable; three new tests in
                      `core_tests.rs` pin the Some-passthrough, the
                      regular-view error wording, and the
                      materialized-view error wording so the two
                      branches cannot drift again.
                    - Resolved issue #204 (trim verbose inline
                      comments added in #202). Replaced the
                      `Dump::require_view_definition` docstring with
                      a two-line note that keeps the non-obvious
                      WHY (`information_schema.views` and
                      `pg_matviews` are privilege-filtered, so NULL
                      means the role can't read the body) and drops
                      the redundant explanation of what the function
                      does — both the function body and the error
                      message already document that.

2026-05-15      v1.0.21

                Bug fixes:
                    - Fixed issue #191 (persistence-ordering silently
                      emits arbitrary order on FK cycles). A mutual
                      FK cycle (`A → B` and `B → A`, valid in
                      PostgreSQL when both FKs are deferrable)
                      flipping persistence in the same direction has
                      no valid SET LOGGED|UNLOGGED order —
                      `SET UNLOGGED A` requires B to already be
                      UNLOGGED and vice versa. Pre-fix
                      `kahn_toposort`'s fallback silently appended
                      cyclic nodes in alphabetical order, the
                      migration emitted the SETs in that order, and
                      PostgreSQL rejected the second SET at apply
                      time with the same `could not change table …
                      to logged/unlogged` error issue #180 was meant
                      to eliminate. Added
                      `kahn_toposort_detect_cycle` returning both
                      the deterministic order and the set of
                      nodes whose in-degree never hit zero; threaded
                      that signal through
                      `topo_order_within_subset_detect_cycle` (the
                      now-only variant — the cycle-blind wrapper was
                      removed since both call sites in
                      `emit_persistence_changes` care about cycles).
                      Adjacency build now also records every
                      `(referrer, target) → TableConstraint` edge
                      so the cycle-break can find the underlying FK
                      objects to drop. When a cycle is present in
                      either SET direction the phase now emits a
                      labeled banner, drops every FK whose endpoints
                      sit in the cyclic set BEFORE the SET
                      statements, runs the SETs (their order no
                      longer matters because the cycle is broken),
                      and re-emits the FKs from their TO-side
                      definitions AFTER the SETs — the same
                      drop-and-recreate pattern PostgreSQL itself
                      recommends for migrations across deferrable
                      cycles. Two new tests:
                      `issue191_persistence_flip_breaks_mutual_fk_cycle`
                      builds a 2-table deferrable cycle in
                      `test_cycle`, flips both LOGGED → UNLOGGED,
                      and asserts the script emits the cycle
                      banner, drops both FKs before any SET, runs
                      both SETs, and re-adds both FKs after; a
                      counter-test
                      (`issue191_persistence_flip_acyclic_chain_does_not_drop_fks`)
                      locks the cycle path to actual cycles so
                      we don't regress and start dropping FKs on
                      every persistence flip. Companion integration
                      fixture `test_order.cycle_a` /
                      `test_order.cycle_b` with mutual
                      `DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED` FKs added to
                      `data/test/schema_a.sql` /
                      `data/test/schema_b.sql` so the round-trip
                      under CI exercises the real
                      `pg_get_constraintdef` deparser output (the
                      drop+SET+re-add path against actual PG, not
                      just the unit-test's assumed string shape).
                      Per PR #198 review feedback: (a) narrowed
                      the cycle set to Tarjan SCCs of size >= 2 —
                      the raw `kahn_toposort_detect_cycle`
                      remainder also catches nodes *blocked by* a
                      cycle (e.g. `C` in `A↔B + A→C`), so dropping
                      every remainder edge was more destructive
                      than needed; added iterative
                      `strongly_connected_components` (Tarjan,
                      work-stack form to avoid recursion-stack
                      blow-up) and the
                      `topo_order_within_subset_detect_cycle` now
                      narrows the cyclic set via SCCs before
                      returning. (b) The cycle re-emit path now
                      stores `TableConstraint` clones (not
                      `(schema, table, name, definition)` tuples)
                      and re-emits via
                      `TableConstraint::get_script()` — preserving
                      `COMMENT ON CONSTRAINT` and any other
                      metadata `get_script()` carries that the raw
                      definition string doesn't. (c) Gated the
                      cycle-break drops AND re-adds behind
                      `use_drop`: when `use_drop=false` the phase
                      emits a loud banner explaining the cycle
                      requires manual intervention (the SETs that
                      follow will fail at apply time without the
                      drops) and comments out the drop and add
                      lines, matching how the rest of the file
                      honours `use_drop=false`. Three additional
                      regression tests:
                      `issue191_pr198_cycle_detection_excludes_blocked_non_cycle_nodes`
                      builds `A↔B + A→C` and asserts the non-
                      cycle `A→C` FK is NOT dropped;
                      `issue191_pr198_use_drop_false_comments_out_cycle_break`
                      asserts the banner mentions `use_drop=false`,
                      drops/re-adds are commented, SETs stay live;
                      `issue191_pr198_cycle_fk_comment_survives_round_trip`
                      attaches a comment to each cycle FK and
                      verifies the re-emitted `COMMENT ON
                      CONSTRAINT` clause survives the drop+SET+
                      re-add sandwich.

                    - Fixed issue #190 (persistence-ordering FK
                      adjacency misses unqualified FK targets in
                      `pg_get_constraintdef` output).
                      `Comparer::emit_persistence_changes`
                      (PR #184 / issue #180) builds an FK adjacency
                      across TO-side tables by parsing each FK
                      constraint's deparsed definition with
                      `parse_fk_referenced_table`. The parser
                      returned `None` when the referenced
                      identifier was unqualified — and
                      `pg_get_constraintdef` routinely emits
                      `REFERENCES table(col)` without the schema
                      prefix when the target is reachable via
                      `search_path` (typically the `public`
                      schema). The missing edge dropped the FK
                      from the adjacency and the SET LOGGED /
                      SET UNLOGGED order fell back to alphabetical,
                      which PostgreSQL rejects with the
                      `could not change table … to logged/unlogged`
                      error whenever a referencing table sorts
                      before the referenced one (`child` <
                      `parent`). CI never caught it because every
                      FK-chain fixture lives in an explicit
                      `test_order` schema. Added an `owner_schema`
                      parameter to `parse_fk_referenced_table` and
                      a fallback that resolves an unqualified
                      target as `(owner_schema, target)` — the
                      simplest and commonest case where the FK
                      target lives in the same schema as the FK
                      itself; the call site in
                      `emit_persistence_changes` now threads
                      `&to_table.schema` through. Two new tests:
                      a unit test that pins the parser fallback
                      (qualified target still wins; unqualified
                      target with quoted/mixed-case owner schema
                      resolves correctly; trailing
                      `ON DELETE CASCADE` clauses don't confuse
                      the boundary scan) and an end-to-end test
                      that builds a `public.child → public.parent`
                      FK chain with unqualified `REFERENCES
                      parent(id)` definitions, flips both tables
                      LOGGED → UNLOGGED, and asserts the child
                      SET UNLOGGED is emitted BEFORE the parent.
                      Companion integration fixture
                      `public.persistence_chain_parent` /
                      `public.persistence_chain_child` added to
                      `data/test/schema_a.sql` /
                      `data/test/schema_b.sql` so the real
                      `pg_get_constraintdef` deparser path is
                      exercised under CI (the role's default
                      `search_path` of `"$user", public` is what
                      causes PG to omit the qualifier — every
                      pre-existing FK-chain fixture lives in an
                      explicit `test_order` schema where the
                      qualifier is always emitted, which is why CI
                      hadn't caught this).
                      Known limitation documented in the parser's
                      doc comment: dump-time per-role /
                      per-database `search_path` settings beyond
                      the owner schema are not captured, so an FK
                      that references a `public` table from a
                      non-`public` schema with `public` on the
                      search_path will still escape this fallback;
                      capturing that would require new dump-side
                      state and is left for a follow-up.

                    - Fixed a non-idempotent migration affecting
                      classical-inheritance children
                      (`CREATE TABLE … INHERITS (parent)`). The
                      dump-side partition-info query
                      (`fetch_partition_info_bulk` in `dump/table.rs`)
                      joined `pg_inherits` without filtering by the
                      parent's `relkind`, so classical-inheritance
                      children landed with `partition_of` set to
                      their parent — the same shape a true partition
                      child has. That mis-classification flowed into
                      `column_type_change_forces_recreate`, which
                      drop+recreated the child for any in-place
                      `ALTER COLUMN TYPE` change. The recreated
                      table silently inherited the current default
                      privileges (`ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN
                      SCHEMA …`), so a second `pgc compare` after
                      applying the migration produced stray
                      `REVOKE … ON TABLE child FROM grantee`
                      statements — the migration was not idempotent.
                      Added an `AND p.relkind = 'p'` predicate to
                      the `LEFT JOIN pg_class p ON p.oid =
                      i.inhparent` join so only declarative-
                      partition parents contribute to `partition_of`;
                      classical-inheritance parents continue to flow
                      through the already-correct `inherits_from`
                      collection, which filters on
                      `pg_partitioned_table` from the opposite
                      direction. Added a regression unit test that
                      builds a Table with `partition_of=None`,
                      `inherits_from=[parent]` and asserts
                      `will_be_dropped_and_recreated` returns false
                      for an in-place column type change, plus a
                      sanity counter-test that a true partition
                      child still forces wholesale recreate.

                    - Fixed issue #188 (Phase 7 doesn't restore
                      secondary dependents of CASCADE-dropped
                      objects). When `DROP FUNCTION ... CASCADE`
                      removed a generated column whose expression
                      referenced the routine, PostgreSQL also
                      silently dropped every index / FK / CHECK /
                      EXCLUDE constraint / RLS policy attached to
                      the column. Phase 7's text-based scanner only
                      matched dependents whose own definition
                      named the routine, so column-anchored
                      secondary dependents were left missing until
                      a second `pgc compare` run noticed the drift.
                      The same gap existed for
                      `TableColumn::get_alter_script`'s
                      virtual-generated-column drop+add path
                      (issue #181 / PR #186). Captured the relevant
                      slice of `pg_catalog.pg_depend` at dump time
                      into a new `Dump::column_dependents` field
                      (additive, `#[serde(default)]` for backward
                      compat with older dumps), populated by a
                      three-branch UNION over pg_depend joined
                      against pg_class / pg_constraint / pg_policy.
                      Added `Comparer::recreate_column_dependents`
                      which walks the graph for a given anchor
                      column and re-emits each TO-side dependent
                      via the existing `*_recreate_block` helpers,
                      with a TO-side gate, partition-child guard,
                      and dedup so a UNIQUE/PK constraint's backing
                      index is not double-emitted. Wired it into
                      Phase 7's generated-column branch and into
                      `compare_tables` after the table alter for
                      columns that route through the
                      `needs_full_recreate` branch (new
                      `TableColumn::would_drop_and_re_add`
                      predicate), the latter wrapped in a labeled
                      `/* Recreate dependents dropped by
                      virtual-column rewrite */` block. Per PR #196
                      review feedback: (a) made
                      `would_drop_and_re_add` the single source of
                      truth — `get_alter_script` now calls the
                      predicate instead of re-deriving the boolean,
                      so Path B detection cannot drift from the
                      emission site; (b) replaced the brittle
                      `alter_script.contains("drop table if exists")`
                      substring-sniff in `compare_tables` with a new
                      `Table::will_be_dropped_and_recreated`
                      predicate that `build_alter_script` itself uses
                      via the shared
                      `column_type_change_forces_recreate` helper —
                      one authoritative decision point for both the
                      grants `recreated_tables` flag and the Path B
                      column-dependent gate; (c) added FK coverage
                      to lock in the asymmetric `conrelid` vs
                      `refobjid` case (FK on a different table
                      referencing the anchor column) plus two-pass
                      ordering in `recreate_column_dependents` so
                      FKs always emit after any UNIQUE/PK target on
                      the same column. New
                      `cascade_col_dep_items` /
                      `cascade_col_dep_children` fixtures in
                      `data/test/schema_a.sql` / `schema_b.sql`
                      cover a plain index, CHECK constraint, UNIQUE
                      constraint, and cross-table FK anchored on a
                      CASCADE-dropped generated column.

                    - Fixed issue #189 (Phase 7 doesn't recreate
                      views silently dropped by `DROP FUNCTION ...
                      CASCADE`). PR #183's Phase 7 restored functional
                      indexes, CHECK constraints, generated columns,
                      column DEFAULTs, and RLS policies after a
                      CASCADE drop, but skipped views — a view whose
                      definition referenced the cascaded routine and
                      was byte-identical between FROM and TO had its
                      hash matched in
                      `compare_routines_and_views`, was never
                      re-emitted, and disappeared from the database
                      until a second `pgc compare` run noticed the
                      drift. Extended
                      `Comparer::recreate_routine_dependents` with a
                      view scan that runs `definition_references_any`
                      over `from_view.definition`, applies the same
                      TO-side gate (PR #186) against
                      `to_view.definition` so views already rewritten
                      by another phase are left alone, and skips views
                      already present in `dropped_views` (Phase 5
                      handles those via the drop-and-recreate path).
                      Regular views emit `CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW`
                      (idempotent: no-op if the view survived CASCADE,
                      a real create if it did not); materialized views
                      emit `CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW IF NOT EXISTS`
                      for the same reason — Phase 7's text-based
                      matcher can false-positive on a view that
                      PostgreSQL never actually dropped, and an
                      unconditional CREATE would fail against a
                      surviving view.

                Enhancements:
                    - Resolved issue #192 (separate tests into
                      `_tests.rs` files): every dump/, comparer/,
                      config/, and utils/ source file that used to
                      embed `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { ... }` at the
                      bottom now declares a sibling test module via
                      `#[cfg(test)] #[path = "<name>_tests.rs"] mod
                      tests;`, with the test bodies moved verbatim
                      into the new `<name>_tests.rs` files. This
                      matches the existing convention already used
                      by `comparer/core.rs` and `comparer/core_tests.rs`
                      and keeps production code files readable
                      without changing what `cargo test` runs.

2026-05-14      v1.0.20

                Bug fixes:
                    - Fixed issue #182 (PG18 NOT ENFORCED CHECK
                      constraints): `TableConstraint::get_script` was
                      appending its own ` not enforced ` clause even
                      when the deparser-supplied `definition` already
                      ended in `NOT ENFORCED`. PG18+
                      `pg_get_constraintdef()` includes the keyword
                      when the constraint is non-enforced, so the
                      generated SQL came out as `… not enforced not
                      enforced ;`. PostgreSQL accepts the duplicate
                      idempotently so it never failed at runtime, but
                      the output was wrong. The fix only appends the
                      keyword when the `clause` (case-insensitively)
                      doesn't already contain it; older-PG dumps and
                      the keyword-less `parts.join`-built path keep
                      working.
                    - Fixed issue #181 (PG18 virtual generated columns):
                      a STORED ↔ VIRTUAL flip with the same generation
                      expression produced no script at all, because
                      `TableColumn::get_alter_script`'s
                      `generated_changed` predicate only considered
                      `is_generated` and `generation_expression` and
                      ignored `generation_type`. Now compares
                      `effective_generation_type` as well, so the flip
                      is detected. And a virtual generated column with
                      a changed expression used to emit
                      `ALTER COLUMN DROP EXPRESSION` + `ALTER COLUMN
                      ADD GENERATED ALWAYS AS (...) VIRTUAL`, both of
                      which PG18 rejects (DROP EXPRESSION is
                      STORED-only; the ADD GENERATED form has no
                      VIRTUAL variant). The fix routes every VIRTUAL
                      participant — flips of `generation_type` in
                      either direction, and expression changes on a
                      VIRTUAL column — through the full
                      `DROP COLUMN` + `ADD COLUMN ... GENERATED ALWAYS
                      AS (...) VIRTUAL|STORED` round-trip, which is
                      the only valid migration PG18 accepts. STORED →
                      STORED expression changes keep the cheaper
                      in-place `DROP EXPRESSION` + `ADD GENERATED ...
                      STORED` form.
                    - Fixed issue #180: `ALTER TABLE ... SET LOGGED|
                      UNLOGGED` was emitted inline with the per-table
                      alter loop in alphabetical order, which
                      PostgreSQL rejects whenever an FK chain flips
                      persistence in the same migration —
                      `SET UNLOGGED` fails if any LOGGED table
                      references the target, `SET LOGGED` fails if the
                      target references any UNLOGGED one. Moved the
                      emission out of `Table::build_alter_script` and
                      into a dedicated
                      `Comparer::emit_persistence_changes` phase that
                      runs at the end of `compare_tables`: tables
                      flipping to UNLOGGED are sorted
                      leaves-before-roots (reverse FK topo), tables
                      flipping to LOGGED are sorted
                      roots-before-leaves (forward FK topo), each via
                      `Self::topo_order_within_subset` over an FK
                      adjacency derived from the TO-side constraint
                      definitions (parsed by
                      `Self::parse_fk_referenced_table`). Owned
                      sequences also get cleaner output:
                      `Sequence::is_only_persistence_change` lets
                      `compare_sequences` skip the entire
                      `ALTER SEQUENCE` for sequences whose only diff
                      is `is_unlogged` and whose owning table is
                      itself flipping persistence (PostgreSQL
                      propagates the
                      `ALTER TABLE SET LOGGED|UNLOGGED` to every owned
                      sequence automatically, so the explicit emit was
                      redundant), and
                      `Sequence::get_alter_script_excluding_persistence`
                      strips just the
                      `ALTER SEQUENCE … SET LOGGED|UNLOGGED` line when
                      the sequence has other diffs to emit but the
                      owning table will still propagate the
                      persistence flip.
                    - Fixed issue #179: when a routine was dropped or
                      its return type / arguments / argument defaults
                      changed, `DROP FUNCTION ... CASCADE` silently
                      removed every dependent linked through
                      `pg_depend` — functional indexes, CHECK
                      constraints, generated columns, column DEFAULTs,
                      and RLS policies — and the comparer never
                      re-emitted them, leaving the database missing
                      those objects until a second `pgc compare` run.
                      Added Phase 7 to
                      `Comparer::compare_routines_and_views`: after the
                      affected functions are recreated, restore each
                      cascaded dependent from its TO-side definition.
                      Generated columns and indexes use non-destructive
                      `ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS` / `CREATE INDEX IF NOT
                      EXISTS`; constraints, policies, and DEFAULTs use
                      idempotent `drop ... if exists` + create or
                      `alter column set default`. The phase honours
                      multiple safety gates: partition-child rules
                      (skip inherited constraints, partition-inherited
                      indexes, and all structural column changes —
                      truly-local child constraints still re-emit);
                      qualified and unqualified detection (deparsers
                      drop the schema qualifier when the function is in
                      `search_path`, both forms must require a `(` call
                      gate so `ON schema.table` / `nextval(... ::
                      regclass)` don't false-positive); UTF-8 boundary
                      walking via `match_indices` for non-ASCII
                      identifiers (`"русское_имя"`); quote-stripped
                      affected-routine names so `quote_ident`-wrapped
                      `"MyFunc"` matches the deparsed `myfunc(`; and a
                      TO-side reference check so dependents already
                      rewritten by `compare_tables()` are left alone
                      (the broken `pg_depend` link means CASCADE leaves
                      them intact, and the IF NOT EXISTS forms make the
                      false-positive case a runtime no-op rather than a
                      destructive drop — overloaded routines collapse
                      together in the text matcher and would otherwise
                      cascade-destroy unrelated indexes / FKs /
                      constraints attached to a still-present column).
                      `Routine::hash()` now includes
                      `arguments_defaults` so a defaults-only change
                      is *detected*. The migration is then emitted
                      via `CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION` — defaults can
                      be updated for the same identity signature and
                      return type without a DROP+CREATE — so
                      `emit_routine_diff` and Phase 7 explicitly
                      exclude defaults-only diffs from the
                      DROP-CASCADE path. The earlier draft of this
                      entry described defaults as a DROP+CREATE
                      requirement; that wording was wrong (PR #187
                      review).
                      Note: a v1.0.19 dump compared against a v1.0.20
                      dump may flag every routine carrying defaults as
                      changed even when the body is identical; rebuild
                      the FROM dump under v1.0.20 to suppress the noise.

2026-05-03      v1.0.19

                Bug fixes:
                    - Refactored comment-related query construction into dedicated
                      helper methods on `Dump`:
                      `build_tables_standalone_query`,
                      `build_regular_views_query`,
                      `build_materialized_views_query`,
                      `build_view_column_comments_query`,
                      `build_sequences_standalone_query`,
                      and on `Table`: `build_indexes_bulk_query`.
                    - Kept `pg_description` joins scoped to relation comments by
                      filtering on `pg_description.classoid = 'pg_class'::regclass`
                      and `pg_description.objsubid = 0` to avoid catalog
                      collisions.
                    - Normalized SQL formatting in the new helper query strings.
                    - `Comparer::compare_grants` no longer runs a duplicate
                      `iter().find(...)` over `from_cols` for every TO column
                      whose own ACL is empty. The first scan was discarded
                      and the second was redone unconditionally; both are
                      now replaced by a single per-table
                      `HashMap<&str, &[String]>` lookup, which also reduces
                      the column-grants emission from O(C^2) to O(C) on
                      wide tables.
                    - `Comparer::mark_serial_columns` now keys
                      `serial_columns` by `(schema, table, column)` tuple
                      instead of a `format!("{}.{}.{}", ...)` string that
                      was parsed back via `splitn(3, '.')`. The string
                      form silently misparsed any identifier containing a
                      literal `.` (legal in PostgreSQL when quoted),
                      leaving the corresponding column unmarked.
                    - `Comparer::compare_sequences` had a dead
                      `dropped_sequences: HashSet<String>` that was
                      checked and updated on every iteration of a loop
                      over `self.from.sequences`. Sequences are unique by
                      `(schema, name)` so the dedupe could never fire;
                      the set and its per-iteration `format!`/`contains`/
                      `insert` calls are removed.
                    - `Comparer::compare_routines` and
                      `Comparer::compare_routines_and_views` no longer
                      clone every `Routine` (each carries the full
                      `source_code` string). The drop path now holds
                      `routines_to_drop: Vec<&Routine>` borrowing into
                      `self.from.routines`. The create/update path —
                      previously forced to clone because
                      `process_target_routine` took `&mut self` and so
                      conflicted with any borrow into `self.from` /
                      `self.to` — is unblocked by refactoring that
                      method into a free associated function
                      `Self::emit_routine_diff(&mut script, use_drop,
                      routine, from_routine)`. Disjoint-field split
                      borrows now allow `&mut self.script` to coexist
                      with `&Routine` borrows out of the dump fields,
                      removing the per-emit clones. Same pattern as the
                      pre-existing `Self::emit_drop`.

                Performance:
                    - `Dump::process` no longer materializes the entire
                      serialized dump as a `String` before handing it to
                      the zip writer. The JSON payload is now streamed
                      into `ZipWriter` via `serde_json::to_writer`
                      through a 256 KiB `BufWriter`, bounding peak
                      memory at the buffer plus zlib's internal state
                      rather than ~2x the uncompressed dump size. The
                      `BufWriter` is required for speed: `to_writer`
                      emits one write per JSON token, and feeding those
                      straight into the deflate stream paid a per-call
                      cost on every one (an early unbuffered version of
                      this change made dumps roughly 10x slower). The
                      write path is exposed as `Dump::write_to_file`,
                      mirroring the existing `Dump::read_from_file` and
                      making the round-trip directly testable.

                Tests:
                    - Added regression tests proving every new query
                      builder includes the `pg_class` classoid filter:
                      `build_tables_standalone_query_filters_by_pg_class`,
                      `build_regular_views_query_filters_by_pg_class`,
                      `build_materialized_views_query_filters_by_pg_class`,
                      `build_view_column_comments_query_filters_by_pg_class`,
                      `build_sequences_standalone_query_filters_by_pg_class`,
                      and `build_indexes_bulk_query_filters_by_pg_class`.
                    - `write_to_file_round_trips_via_read_from_file`
                      builds a Dump with schemas, extensions, tables,
                      views, sequences, and routines, writes it via the
                      new streaming path, reads it back, and asserts
                      every collection size and a few content fields
                      match.
                    - `compare_column_grants_dispatches_per_column_acl_correctly`
                      builds a table with three columns whose effective
                      `from_acl` differs (kept / revoked / newly
                      granted) and verifies that the per-table
                      column-ACL HashMap dispatches each column to the
                      right diff outcome — guarding against off-by-one
                      mistakes that single-column tests would miss.
                    - `mark_serial_columns_handles_dotted_identifier_names`
                      drives the `mark_serial_columns` path with a
                      schema, table, and column name that all contain a
                      literal `.`, asserting that the new tuple key
                      still locates the target column. The pre-fix
                      `splitn(3, '.')` parser would have failed on this
                      input.

2026-04-25      v1.0.18

                Bug fixes:
                    - Fixed grants for former owners after an owner change
                      (two-stage fix). The original v1.0.17 behaviour
                      filtered both old and new owners as implicit
                      privilege holders, silently dropping explicit grants
                      to the previous owner from the diff. The first
                      attempt switched to filtering only the `to` owner —
                      which exposed a non-idempotent oscillation: PG
                      materialises an implicit-owner ACL row in
                      `pg_class.relacl` (e.g. `pgc_owner_from=arwdDxt/
                      pgc_owner_from`) and treating it as a regular
                      grantee made the comparer emit a long REVOKE on
                      first run, then GRANT statements on every
                      subsequent run. The fix splits owner filtering into
                      asymmetric `from_owners` / `to_owners` lists that
                      model the effect of `ALTER ... OWNER TO`: the FROM
                      owner's implicit-owner row is stripped from the
                      FROM side (because `ALTER OWNER` removes it) and
                      the TO owner's implicit row is stripped from the
                      TO side (because `ALTER OWNER` recreates it). When
                      ownership does not change both lists hold the same
                      single role, collapsing back to the original
                      behaviour. Column ACLs (`pg_attribute.attacl`) pass
                      an empty `from_owners` because `ALTER TABLE OWNER`
                      does not touch column-level grants — explicit
                      column grants to a former owner persist and must
                      still be revoked under `full` mode.
                    - `FOREIGN TABLE` ACL parsing now uses the same
                      privilege set as `TABLE` (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE,
                      DELETE, TRUNCATE, REFERENCES, TRIGGER, MAINTAIN).
                      Previously, foreign-table privileges were parsed
                      against the default minimal set, which dropped
                      legitimate privileges from the diff. Foreign-table
                      grant emission now uses `GRANT ... ON TABLE` rather
                      than the invalid `GRANT ... ON FOREIGN TABLE`
                      syntax (PostgreSQL has no `ON FOREIGN TABLE` form
                      for GRANT/REVOKE — applying the previous diff
                      raised `syntax error at or near "TABLE"`).
                    - Foreign-table grants are now idempotent for newly
                      created foreign tables. PostgreSQL applies default
                      table privileges (`pg_default_acl.defaclobjtype =
                      'r'` covers foreign tables too) at CREATE time, so
                      a TO-only foreign table inherits the FROM database's
                      default ACL on the freshly created object. Without
                      adjusting the comparer's effective `from_acl`, a
                      second compare run after applying the migration
                      emitted spurious `REVOKE` statements for those
                      auto-applied privileges. The foreign-table grant
                      path now mirrors the regular-table path: under
                      `full` mode, new foreign tables use the FROM
                      default-table ACL as the effective `from_acl` so
                      any required revokes are emitted in the same diff
                      run.
                    - ACL parser now handles quoted role names with
                      embedded `=`, `/`, or escaped `""` quotes (e.g.
                      `"weird=name"=r/owner`). The previous naive
                      `find('=')` / `find('/')` split misparsed these and
                      silently corrupted the grantee/grantor strings.
                    - Dependency-scan needles in the comparer (used to
                      decide drop/create order for views and routines)
                      now strip quotes when matching against the
                      quote-stripped haystack flavour. Dump fields
                      populated via `quote_ident` literally embed `"` for
                      mixed-case identifiers, and the previous code could
                      not match a quoted needle against an unquoted
                      reference in a definition body — silently losing
                      real dependency edges.
                    - `--use-drop=false` is now honoured uniformly for
                      collations, text-search configurations, text-search
                      dictionaries, casts, operators, default privileges,
                      publications, subscriptions, foreign data wrappers,
                      foreign servers, and user mappings. These eleven
                      object kinds previously *omitted* the DROP entirely
                      under `use_drop=false`; they now emit a commented
                      DROP, matching the behaviour already in place for
                      tables, views, types, enums, sequences, routines,
                      rules, event triggers, and foreign tables.

                Reliability:
                    - `current_setting('server_version_num')` is now
                      fetched once at the start of `Dump::fill` and
                      threaded into the per-object-kind futures. Previously
                      it was queried separately by the type and table
                      branches, doubling the round-trip cost on every dump.
                    - Unparseable ACL items are now logged with a
                      `Warning:` line. Previously a silently-skipped
                      grantee would simply disappear from the diff if a
                      future PostgreSQL version introduced an aclitem
                      syntax pgc did not yet recognise.
                    - `Config::load` now warns when `FROM_HOST` or
                      `TO_HOST` is empty. The same-target warning gated
                      on both hosts being non-empty, so a config with
                      missing host keys silently fell back to defaults.
                    - `get_schemas` collapsed an `if let Err` followed by
                      `result.unwrap()` into a single `?`-chain, removing
                      a redundant unwrap on a Result that was just
                      pattern-matched.

                Internals:
                    - SEQUENCE ACL parsing explicitly documents that
                      MAINTAIN ('m', PG17+) is filtered out — MAINTAIN is
                      only valid on tables, views, materialised views,
                      and foreign tables, never sequences.
                    - `to_owners` semantics are documented on
                      `generate_column_grants_script` (extending the
                      doc already added to `diff_acls`): current owners
                      are skipped because they have implicit access;
                      former owners stay in the diff as ordinary grantees.

                Tests:
                    - New regression tests covering owner-change grant
                      propagation, explicit grants to former owners under
                      `full` mode, and the foreign-table privilege set.
                    - Quoted-grantee ACL parsing tests for embedded `=`,
                      `/`, escaped `""`, and quoted grantor.
                    - Owner-unchanged with explicit owner grant in TO
                      (must be filtered as implicit).
                    - SEQUENCE with stray MAINTAIN bit (must be silently
                      dropped, not emitted as an invalid GRANT).
                    - Column-grant former-owner full-mode test where TO
                      has *no* explicit column ACL (must still revoke
                      from former owner without grants to the new owner).
                    - Dependency-scan needle tests covering both
                      quoted-against-unquoted and quoted-against-quoted
                      references.

2026-04-23      v1.0.17

                Bug fixes:
                    - PostgreSQL version agnostic handling of attstattarget
                      attribute.
                    - Replaced double `.unwrap()` in the dump serialization
                      path with proper error propagation so a `serde_json`
                      failure surfaces as a readable error instead of a
                      panic.
                    - `compare_types` no longer emits a stray
                      `-- Multirange … is created automatically` comment for
                      new range types. PostgreSQL auto-creates the
                      companion multirange row when `CREATE TYPE … AS RANGE`
                      runs, so the comparer now skips new-in-`to`
                      multiranges in the CREATE branch (symmetric to the
                      existing skip in the DROP branch). The skip is
                      deliberately scoped to the new-type branch only — an
                      existing multirange whose owner, comment, or ACL
                      differs between dumps still reaches
                      `get_alter_script` and emits the corresponding
                      `COMMENT ON TYPE` / `ALTER TYPE … OWNER TO`. This
                      was the leading cause of diffs looking "empty" on
                      schemas that added a new range type.

                Reliability:
                    - Added `FILL_SIBLING_BRANCH_COUNT` constant with a
                      compile-time arity guard against the outer `try_join!`
                      in `Dump::fill`, plus a runtime warning when
                      `--max-connections` / `MAX_CONNECTIONS` is below the
                      number of parallel dump branches (pool starvation
                      would otherwise go unnoticed).
                    - `Config::load` now warns when `FROM_*` and `TO_*`
                      resolve to the same host+port+database+schema, which
                      almost always means a typo in the config file and
                      silently produces an empty diff.

                Tooling:
                    - CI build job runs against both Rust 1.88.0 (floor)
                      and 1.95.0 (matches the Dockerfile toolchain), so the
                      shipped Docker image toolchain is CI-validated.
                    - Dockerfile `version` / `org.opencontainers.image.version`
                      labels are now filled from `ARG PGC_VERSION` wired
                      through GitHub Actions from `app/Cargo.toml` — no
                      more hard-coded version skew on release.

                UX / Docs:
                    - Fixed copy-pasted `--server` help text on `--port`
                      and `--scheme`; documented the `SIMILAR TO` matching
                      behaviour of `--scheme` (e.g. `public|app`) and
                      expanded `--grants-mode` to describe ignore /
                      addonly / full explicitly.
                    - Added explanatory comments at every
                      `recreated_tables.insert()` site documenting the
                      coupling with `compare_grants` default-ACL handling
                      for dropped-and-recreated tables.

                Tests:
                    - New buffer-ordering tests pin the emission order of
                      the comparer's post-script buffers:
                      sequence_post_script → type_post_script →
                      enum_post_script → trigger_post_script.
                    - Replaced `.find().unwrap()` assertions in the
                      clear-script tests with a helper that panics with
                      the full script, so ordering-test failures are
                      actionable.
                    - New multirange regression tests covering both sides
                      of the fix:
                      `compare_types_multirange_not_created_independently`
                      (symptom fix) and
                      `compare_types_multirange_comment_change_still_emits_alter`
                      (guards against over-broad skipping — a metadata
                      ALTER on an existing multirange must still emit).

2026-04-20      v1.0.16

                Performance:
                    - Eliminated N+1 query pattern in the dump path. Per-table
                      metadata (columns, indexes, constraints, triggers, policies,
                      partition info, definitions) is now fetched via seven
                      schema-wide queries instead of seven queries per table,
                      collapsing `7 × N` round-trips into 7 on remote connections.
                    - Prelower routine source code once per routine in O(n²)
                      dependency scans inside the comparer (three routine loops
                      plus the routine-and-view phase). Previously each inner
                      iteration re-lowercased the full source text.
                    - Prelower view definitions once per view in the view-drop
                      dependency scan for the same reason.
                    - Precompute foreign-key constraint definitions (lowercase
                      plus quote-stripped-lowercase) once across the FROM dump
                      before scanning dropped tables, removing repeated
                      `to_lowercase()` / `chars().filter()` work from the
                      O(T × T × C) inner loop.

                Bug fixes:
                    - Config file values containing `=` (e.g. passwords with
                      base64 `=` padding) no longer cause `Invalid configuration
                      line` panics. The parser now splits on the first `=` only.
                    - `Config::new` now has a non-panicking counterpart,
                      `Config::load(&str) -> Result<Config, String>`, which
                      `main.rs` uses to report malformed configs cleanly instead
                      of aborting with a Rust panic.

                Changes:
                    - Default `MAX_CONNECTIONS` / `--max-connections` raised from
                      8 to 16. With the new bulk-query dump path the tool fires
                      up to ~18 concurrent introspection queries; the previous
                      default starved the pool on high-latency links.
                    - README documentation for `MAX_CONNECTIONS` updated to
                      reflect the new bulk-query model.

2026-04-17      v1.0.15

                Features:
                    - UNLOGGED tables support with CREATE UNLOGGED TABLE and ALTER TABLE SET LOGGED/UNLOGGED.
                    - Storage parameters (reloptions) support with WITH (...) and ALTER TABLE SET/RESET.
                    - REPLICA IDENTITY support (DEFAULT, NOTHING, FULL) with ALTER TABLE.
                    - FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY support with ALTER TABLE FORCE/NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY.
                    - Classical table inheritance (INHERITS) support.
                    - Typed tables (OF type) support.
                    - Per-column statistics target (SET STATISTICS) with ALTER support.
                    - Function/procedure COST and ROWS clauses.
                    - Function SUPPORT clause.
                    - Function TRANSFORM FOR TYPE clause.
                    - New object types with full CREATE/ALTER/DROP/compare support:
                        - Rules (`CREATE OR REPLACE RULE`, `DROP RULE`, comparison by definition hash).
                        - Collations (libc/ICU/default providers, locale, deterministic flag).
                        - Text search configurations (parser, token-to-dictionary mappings).
                        - Text search dictionaries (template and options).
                        - Foreign data wrappers (handler/validator functions, options).
                        - Foreign servers (type, version, FDW reference, options).
                        - User mappings (user-to-server with options).
                        - Publications (`FOR ALL TABLES` or per-table, publish operations).
                        - Subscriptions (connection string, publication list, enable/disable).
                        - Operators (procedure, left/right types, commutator, negator, HASHES/MERGES).
                        - Casts (function/binary-coercible/I-O, assignment/implicit context).
                        - Event triggers (ddl_command_start/end, sql_drop, table_rewrite, filter tags, enable states).
                    - Default privileges comparison (`ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES`) with per-role
                      and per-schema scoping, GRANT/REVOKE diff generation.
                    - Column-level ACLs: per-column GRANT/REVOKE (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, REFERENCES).

                Bug fixes:
                    - Removed `has_rules` from table hash computation. Rules are compared
                      independently; including them in the hash caused spurious view drops
                      when a rule was added or removed.
                    - Added CASCADE to DROP TYPE statements. Dependent objects (e.g.
                      multirange types) could prevent type drops without it.
                    - Fixed idempotency of grants for recreated objects. Tables that are
                      dropped and recreated during migration (e.g. partition key change)
                      now use the default privilege ACL as the effective source ACL in
                      `compare_grants`, so auto-applied grants are properly revoked in a
                      single diff run.
                    - Fixed idempotency of grants for dropped and recreated views. Views
                      dropped as dependencies and then recreated now use the default
                      privilege ACL instead of an empty ACL, preventing persistent REVOKE
                      statements on subsequent runs.

